      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>ILLUSTRATED FASHIONS.</head>
        <p>ILLUSTRATED FASHIONS.</p>
        <p>Lace is still one of the most lovely and most popular trimmings, and forms a dainty adinnot to the smartest toilettes and hats. It graces also the fur coat and necklet, and nestles among the furry tails which ornament the newest muffs, which, by the by, Erre of the vary large and deep order favoured by oar grandmothers Although a mellow tone is correct for old lace, soiled should not be worn. I heard the other day of a very simple method for "dry cleaning" lace at home. This was to spread the soiled lace smoothly and carefully on a piece of clean white paper, and cover it with calcined mag- nesia. Another piece of white paper is then put over the top, and the sheets are then placed between the leaves of a book for several days. All it needs afterwards is a good shake to re- move the powdered magnesia. If the lace is very much soiled this process will have to be repeated, when the result should be eminently satisfactory, and the lace ready for wearing.</p>
        <p>The pretty blouse depicted is made of black spotted net over white silk trimmed and ap- pliqued with lace, while a large bow and ends of pink silk are arranged as a finish to the bodice. The sleeves which terminate just below the elbow are finished by a deep full frill of net trimmed at the lower edge with an applique of the creamy lace. A bodice made in this style could be worn with an old black satin skirt, the waist being finished by a broad pink satin ribbon folded so as to come narrow in front, while at the back a smart bow is arranged with two long ends falling to ._rt few inches of the</p>
        <p>BLOUSE.</p>
        <p>tern of the skirt. A very good plan for smart- ening up an old gown is to arrange a series of narrow net frills round the hem, making these -come further up the back than the front. Of course, a jetted or sequinned overdress is more easily adjusted and looks handsomer, but it is likewise much more expensive a matter for serious consideration to many. A handsome chenille fringe is also considered very modish, while more strikingly beautiful are those of jet, pearl, or iridiscent beads arranged round the décolletage and on, the skirt. Another pretty style for an evening gown is to have a simulated panel down the front of the skirt with looped up scarves or draperies across the front, each edged with a drooping fringe. Pearl tassels make a pretty finish to a white or pale cream-coloured toilette.</p>
        <p>The pretty coiffure sketched .is one much favoured this season, the hair being arranged in deep waves on the bead and brought down to the nape of the neck behind where it is arranged in several thick twists with sometimes</p>
        <p>ING COIFFI7IIE.</p>
        <p>a loose curl, and tied with a broad black satin or velvet ribbon. The result is very charming, and most becoming to the possessors of blond or auburn hair. For the brunette the ribbon should be coloured and a bright crimson for preference, as this tone makes a particularly pleasing • contrast to dusky locks. Velvet is quite the material par excellence this winter, and is to be met with in the form of shaped bands, separate flounces, or appliques, as well as forming whole gowns. It is a particularly be- coming material, and being at once soft and rich looking requires little or no trimming un- less it be some fur. Corduroy velvet is con- sidered very smart for outdoor wear, and it is a harder and better wearing material than the other. Velvet, however, is a material for the rich only, as it must be of good quality to wear and look well, and the cost of a really good velvet toilette is considerable. Two golden rules to observe in selecting anything are never to buy anything that is startling or conspicuous mes s you have a long purse, and avoid any- thing that is too smart or remarkable to go with the rest of your toilette, as it will make the whole look shabby.</p>
        <p>The smart winter costume for a little girl illustrated is made of pastel blue serge, the skirt and coat being to match. The cape - like acllar is trimmed with a band of lace and a</p>
        <p>depending full ,embroidery, while a big bow and long ends of cream satin ribbon finish the front. The hat is also cream coloured and of a rough surfaced felt, and is trimmed by some soft silk ribbon and a curling ostrich feather of the same tone. The whole is a very becoming outdoor eoetume for a little girl of six to eight years aid. Of course, it would look quite as pretty expre.sed altogether in cream coloured materials, but it is not every one who can afford to dress their children in such delicate colours during the worst af the winter. Nothing is more un- becoming to a child than soiled orstained finery, a tact ignored by too many mothers. Better have a simple coat and skirt of some warm tone soon ae crimson than a dirty white. Children nowadays are dressed in a much more picturesque style Oaan of yore,</p>
        <p>CO4IIETTE.</p>
