      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The excellent health which their Majesties the king and Queen are new enjoying is not unfortunately shared by all the members of their faraily. The Duchess of Fife has been very unwell in Scotland this autumn, and it was only quite recently that she was permitted to undertake the journey to the South. Princess Victoria who is greatly subject to neuralgia, has also been I far from well, and Princess Charles of Denmark, who is a martyr to the same complaint, has been in the hands of a specialist, undergoing a "rest cure," at Buckingham Palace.</p>
        <p>Surrounded by London, it scarcely eeerns the best place for a "rest cure " and it was regarded by Queen Victoria as not the roest suitable residence for anyone subject to rheumatism or kindred ailments. But it is easy to obtain perfect quietness and some of the rooms overlooking the grounds, which were occupied by till King during his illness, and more recently by Princess Charles, are all that could be desired in this way, and might be from their outlook miles away in the country. The King of Portugal who has only recently recovered from an attack of influenza, is too stout to be healthy. It is thought that there is nathing like worry to make one thin, but, though the King of Portugal is seldom free from political_w-cries, it has no effect in reducing his weight. His Majesty nevertheless leads a very active life, and he is only just upon forty years of age. He is not onlj a dead stet and a good Sportsman, bu% possesses considerable literary ability, and is an excel- lent judge of art. He is married to Frincess Ansdlia of Orleans, who is almcst as versatile in her hobbies as the German Emperor. She is a great expert with the gun, and tier knowledge and powers of swimming have enabled her at different times, even at the risk of her own life, to rescue her husband's subjects from drown- ing. She is also credited with quite a professional knowledge of medicine, but there are stories that her doctoring of small ailments has not always been fully appreciated by members of the Royal Household. Mrs Joseph Chamberlain who will accompany her distinguished husband en his visit to South Africa, has a rather quiet and reserved manner. Such a disposition however generally carries the greater influence and it is a rather fortunate one in the wife of the Colonial Secretary who is supposed to have no appreciation for 'the politically minded woman who affects to guide the idestinies of the Empire by prssiding over a saloa. Though Mrs Chamberlain is an American by birth, she has never aspired to become a great hostess in a political sense, but whilst she is not a great Society personage, she is very popular, especially in Birmingham, where she constantly entertains her husband's friends. Several fashionable weddings are announeed to take place during the next few weeks, and that of Mr St. 7ohn Bredrick and Miss Madeleine Stanley is expected to take place before the end of the year. Miss Stanley. whip is a tall and handsome girl, is just twenty years younger than her future husband, who is in his forty- sixth year. She is the daughter of Lady Jeune, her father being Colonel John Stanley, Lady Jeune's first husband, who'died in 1878. Miss Stanley is a familiar figure in these circles which include the most brilliant representatives of politics, literature, and art, and like her mother, she takes an active interest in the political and social questions of the day. During the past few years, so many occupations have been opened in this country to women, that oee is rather surprised to read that until just now no lady has started in Paris, the business of a maker ef dresses, mantles, and millinery. It has bsen generally understood that the great businesses of this description in Paris were controlled by men, but one hardly supposed that the monopoly was so complete as it appears to have been. In the West End of London there are many women who derive large incomes from the business of dressmakers and milliners, and one would have supposed that with greater reason such occupations would be open to women in Paris.</p>
        <p>With the exception of evening, or quite dressy after- noon underskirts, French linen batiste is being em- ployed for these garments. We are all familiar with the spectacle an underskirt presents, after it bag been taken for a constitutional, or a tour of the shops, on a muddy Gay. One may brush and brush when the mud is dry, but the stains remain. and a good silk skirt is often ruined in this way. With batiste all this is changed. A journey to the laundress, and a skirt of this fabric—it is firm, serviceable, and does not easily soil—bas the appearance of a new garment. These underskirts, are carefully fitted abcut the waist and hips, well clear the ground, and the frills, usually four in number, and about three or four inches in width, are edged with lam—With regard to the colours chosen, these it should be remembered must match the hat.and blouse Cr front, with which they are to be worn. - . _</p>
        <p>The fashionable belts point upward at the back, and downward in front,and are particularly becoming. They are built of divers fabrics, but soft silks and satins are perhaps first favourites. The gorgeous tinsel arrange- ments are no longer seen, and all startling contrasts for belts are avoided by leading dressmakers. For quite young kirl'e wear, dainty belts formed of a trellis work of tiny flowers are to be had, the most successful being in pale colours, rt. white—a trail, to he arranged about the deeollstage, Sr a balers, to match, accompanies the belt, the "set" being a charming finish to a young girl's toilette.</p>
        <p>There appears to be quite a craze for shaded goods. Apart from dress fabrics,there are shaded ribbons,whieh are being employed so extensively just now ; shaded hosiery which is not to be despised when the colours match those of the costume ; and shaded foetgear,whieh under any circumstances is best avoided. The cr ,zs has, happily, net extended to gloves. Sessde, in pale shades, black, and white.is the correct wear, and heavy points are net permissible. The London Needlework Guild distributes every year a very large number of useful articles amongst different ebarities. The Guild has the generous support of the Royal Family, and at its recent exhibition at the Imperial Institute, there were on view large conttibus Gone from the King, the Prince and Princess of Wales, and other Rival personages. Altogether there were nearly 60,000 articles of wearing apparel, mostly warm woollen garments, and articles of household use. The Princess of Wales seat a very large number, and alsa some contributions from her children, including lime blue knitted cuffs from Princess Mary Victoria, and some bundles of stockings from the little Princes. APPLE SIIRPETSE.—Pare and core some good cooking apples, simmer in water until tender, sweeten, beat, and when cool place them in a glass dish and cover with boiled custard. When this has set, ornament the edgec with ratafia biscuits and halved glace cherries, arrang- ing a device of thesame in tho centre, and serve. MADGE.</p>
        <p>James Henry Pickup,the Bury J.P., who was charged with extensive frauds, was sentenced at the Manchester A93IZPS On Wednesday to seven years' penal servitude. No evidence was offered against his son, who was dis- charged. Mr Justice Jell, who imposed so many long sentences of penal servitude on the previous day, on Wednesday sentenced a housebreaker to five years, and a Burnley man who had assaulted his wife with a hamm2r to seven years. A pathetic story was related at a Birmingham inquest rn Wednesday. A cycle worker named Capewell had in early life accidently shot and killed his mother with a toy pistol He had brooded over this so much that he committed suicide. Deceased was engaged to b married, and had said to his financse, " We can never mate. I killed my mother, but I never intended it."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>FATALITY AT DALLAM</head>
        <p>THE FATALITY AT DALLAM</p>
        <p>FORGE. Inquest and Verciict.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, conducted an bequest at the Warrington Police Station on Tuesday afternoon touelaing the death of Daniel Speed, aged forty-two, who !edged in Foundry-street, and was killed whilst following his employment at Dallam Forge last Friday morning.—Major R3e, Inspector of Factories, watched the proceedings on behalf of the Horne Office, and Mr Taylor, solicitor, (Messra Greenall and C 0.,) represented Me.srs Pearson and Knowles. John Roberts, who lodged with the deceased, said Speed was forty-two years of age and was a labourer at Dallana Forge. His mother, who was 81 years of age, resided in Liverpool. Witness also worked at the forge and was present when thi accident happened although he did not actually witness it. At about 9.15 some workmen were about to hoist a principal from the ground ty means of a derrick,and deceased was folding the steering line. A number of steel plates were stacked up near the principal but were tilted from the principal. I travelling crane was being used to hoist other plates to the stack. Witness was working on the roof of the building and did at see the accident. On hearing a noise ho leaked round sad saw that the stack of plates bad fallen over and crushed the deceased against the principal. Deceased was taken to the Infirraary on an ambulance but was dead when ha reacted the institution.</p>
        <p>John Naylor, the driver of the crane, said there were tenets, plates in position, and witness was heisting others to them in pairs. Deceased was standing between the stack of plates and the principal and was holding the steering rope. Witnest received orders from the "plater" to hoist a pair of plates and proceeded to do se. Otte of the plates which was being hoisted caught against one of the stacked plates and caused them all to fall over. The plate which brought about the accident was sewed with a belt and shackle, which, however, were not in the centre. This caused the plate to tilt when boirrs hoisted. Witness thought that if the bolt and shackle had seen fastened in the centre the accident would not have happened, Walter Carrick, the " plater," said it was his duty to fasten the bolt and shackle to the plates. As regarded the plate in question he was not able to Ax the bolt and shackle in the centre, es he could not pierce a bole there. Witness gave the signal for the plates to be heisted and this was dame. Owing to the plate in question tilting, one end caught on a billet six inchss above the ground before the other end touchel the ground. Tnis caused the plate to fall over and the bolt and shackle struck one of the stacked plates and they all fell over.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
