      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <p>H.M.S. ANSON, FROM WHInII THE FIRST WELCOME TO TIIE FArstril WAS crivmN</p>
        <p>His Inverial Majesty the Emperor of Germany arrived in England last Saturday, reaching Port Victoria exactly as arranged and carrying out his trimming's programme with wonderful punctuality. The Kaiser travelled on his steam yacht, the Ihkenzollern, escorted by the Nymphe and the Sleipner, which were joined by half-a-dozen British torpedo-destroyers, as the little fleet en- tered British waters. Ilis Majesty received a royal salute, and the ships under Vice-Admiral Mark- ham, Commander-in-Chief at the Nore, were all gaily dressed in bunting. The German Ambassador. Count , Wolff Metternich boarded the Imperial yacht, and was followed by Lord Churchill, who is the Kaiser's Lard-in-Waiting for the visit, and other distinguished people. The journey to Shorncliffe ;was made .via Dover in a specially constructed royal train, of which the centre saloon was magnificent. Lord Roberts, as Com- mander-in-Chief, with ,his Staff met the royal visitor at the station and escorted him to the parade ground, on which he was to review his own regiment, the Ist Royal Dragoons. The scene was considerably marred from the point of view of pleasure and effectiveness by the terrible storm of wind and rain that was beating down: but other- wise the function passed off very pleasantly, the Kaiser riding down the line on a magnificent charger and then taking the salute at the march past, which was smartly and quickly executed. The Kaiser made a cordial speech of welcome and called for three cheers for King Edward, which were warmly given: and then Lord Basing, as Colonel, replied, and three equally enthusiastic cheers were given for the Kaiser. The Imperial Colonel-in-Chief then lunched with the officers of the regiment, and made another most pleasing little speech in acknowledging the toast of his health, doubling his contribution to the funds of the widows and orphans of the regiment. The Kaiser left Shorncliffe by special train and reached Liverpool Street at half-past three, and proceeding direct to Wolferton, arrived at his destination just after six, when he was met and affectionately greeted by his uncle King Edward. His Majesty's birthday party at Sandringham to meet the Kaiser included the Prince of Wales, • the Prime Minister, the Colonial Secretary, the Secretary for War, the German Ambassador, the British Ambassador at Berlin (Sir F. Lascelles) aad many other distinguished persons.</p>
        <p>That marvellous violinist, Herr lan Kubelik, who on Sunday last had the inestimable honour of delighting the monarchs of two of the world's most powerful nations, is a genuine example of how true merit may meet with its due reward. Kubelik was born at Michle, in Bohemia, in the year 1880, his father having no better station in life than that of a hard-worked and none too well paid market gardener. Young lan, however, had in him the genius that no amount of poverty</p>
        <p>TIM NINO AND KAISER AT filiAlet ON SUNDAY, ,cauld have suppressed, and from a very early period of his career showed a deep interest in anything musical. It was the life's aim of his father to develop the latent talent he felt fpnfident his son possessed, and the old Tan die not rest until lan was a student of Prague Conservatoire. Entering the latter at the age of 12 he quickly showed his remarkable gifts. and his tutors, seeing what scope for practical guidance there was in him were not loath to avail themselves of the opportunity of having to their credit the teaching ef one whom they recognised as a coming master. lan's progress at scohol was as quick and brilliant as his reception by the world has been, awl now at the age of 22 he stands, in the opinion of most, the premier violiniQt. He gave recitals before dis- tinguished audiences at the early age of 16, and all criti' s were unanimous in pronouncing his debut the beginning of a wonderful success Since then he has travelled through the whole of Europa, and is violinist of the Royal Court of Roumania, Commander of the Sava Order of .ervia, commander of the Gregory Order from the ;L:OI,P, and t.n honorary member of the Philharmonic aocietieeof London and Prague. Though it is not considered possible to employ the Somlis in this latest effort to silence the Mad Mullah in the same proportion as heretofore, it is gratifying to find, among the many disquieting rumours from Somaliland that there is every reasop to rely upon native assistance. The accom- panying, sketch throws an interesting light on the &amp;maga war methods at close quarters. It shown how Somali troopers still practice the arts of war</p>
        <p>LAND NATIVE METHODS OF which their forefathers have known Although the levy have been instructed of the rifle, the spearmen have played in fairly recent engagements with the f, Mullah, and ,uch work as defendin, they have be, ad very useful. Offers assistance in O. npaign are not wanting. A promise of 500 Colonials has been received from CoTeel Royston, to say nothing of the offer of an Indian eamel corps. The African explorer, Signor SWISS. who is an adopted eon Of the Sultan of</p>
