      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <p>SANDRINGHAM HOUSE.</p>
        <p>EVENTS OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>MABRITALL</p>
        <p>TIJE KAISER AS BRITISH FIELD-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <p>LOWTHER• CASTLE, WHERE TUB KAISER PROPOSES TO GO OX THE 15TH INST.</p>
        <p>Mijertein, States that England certainly ought to took for the co-operation of the various Sultans In Somaliland, British and Italian. Signor Sylos about to proceed to Somaliland, where he will idace his services at the disposal of the British. rommander. The Foreign Office has now awakened to the importance of making a vigorous campaign tgainst the Mullah. Colonel Swayne has left for London to state exactly the position and require- ments. and until his arrival little improvement in the situation can be expected. In any case, pro- tracted operations are inevitablei and in the mean- time the Mullah is profiting by the delay. It is estimated that he possesses 2,000 rifles, a Maxim, and large quantities od ammunition. His men's rifles :are mostly of nrodern French make, coming from Jibutil, but there are some Martinis and a few Lee-Metfords and Mausers among them. Besides his riflemen, the Mullah is accompanied by 15,000 spearraen, and' has at hand reserve supplies of camels and sheep. Samoliland is a land of possibilities. Its present exports amount annually to considerably over a million pounds. Its area is 68,000 miles, and it has a population of 200,000.</p>
        <p>Memorials to the great and good Queen, who for so many years swayed and controlled the destinies of this great Empire with such marvellous success, are springing up on all sides and all parts of the wide confines of her erstwhile dominions. One of the latest has been subscribed by the officers and men of the Ahmadnagar Constabulary, and was recently unveiled in that district : and will always stand as a great token of the love and loyalty of that far</p>
        <p>ME INDIAN QUEEN VICTORIA MEMORIAL. distant district. In the absence of the commis- sioner of the divisions Mr. R. A. Lamb, C.1.E., the collector, unveiled the memorial in .the presence of a large gathering of prominent Europeans and leading citizens. In his speech, Mr. Lamb con- gratulated the police force on the great accom- plishment of having subscribed a memorial entirely upon their own account, and praised the generosity which had brought about such a satisfactory result. The force was the principal organ of Government in the district, he continued, and therefore, it was peculiarly appropriate that they should be the first to erect a memorial to the late Queen Victoria in the district of Nagar. The memorial consists of a very handsome bust, pedestal and canopy, and is decidedly an artistic success. It was designed by Mr. Benson, the Executive Engineer, A.D., and constructed by K. S. Eduljee Rustoniji. On the day of unveiling it was draped with a large Union Jack, and guarded on either side by a Mohammedan and a Hindoo constable. The function has given the liveliest satisfaction throughout India, a sentiment which will be echoed in every part of the Empire, in which the memory of Queen Victoria, her life and her deeds, will ever remain fresh.</p>
        <p>livery day the feeling of hostility between ••olice and public seems to grow worse, and thesct is little doubt that the police force as a wholeare losing caste by the actions of some of its individual members. No class of the community can do se much good or so much harm as the police organisation of the country: and it should be above reproach. Only recently a constable was sentenced to five years penal servitude at the last</p>
        <p>ZWIS, ..11E COE3THE CONSTABLE CONCERNED. AS HE APPEARED IN COURTJ Old Bailey sessions for perjury and trumping up a charge against a peacefurcitizen : and all over the country come tales of the prejudiced treatme4 Of motorists, and some very dubious evidence is given in case after case. Last week a very strange case was heard at the Westminster Police Court before Mr. Horace Smith, in which a costermonger called Stow, was charged with assaulting the police in the person of Police-constable Evans, of the division. The case called forth a con- siderable amount of contradictory evidence; but the main point was whether Stow assaulted Evans, or Evans assaulted Stow. Stow's tale was that he returned from an errand and found his wife bleeding on the ground from a cut over the eye with P.C. Evans looking on. The policeman advised him to take her indoors, and, as he was doing so %truck him a blow on the back of the head with his truncheon. Shortly after wards he was arrested by several constables and taken to the station, where he was charged with assaulting Evans. Later on another policeman, ac-ording te Stow, tried to scrare the matter on Evans' behalf but he refused. In consequence of the evidsnce, Mr. Horace Smith discharged the prisoner for want of proof to convict him, and granted a summons against P.C. Evans, which was im- mediately served, remarking parenthetically that he would lay the evidence adduced during the trial before the Chief Commissioner of Police. The case has now got to a stage which is evoking considerable public interest.</p>
