      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>rilE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 14, 1902.</head>
        <p>rilE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 14, 1902.</p>
        <p>HOW I WRITE MY PLAYS.</p>
        <p>A Chat With Mr. Laurence Rodgers.</p>
        <p>Mr Laurance Rodgers, author of that well-known success, "Susan," is an American who haa lately made London his home, living in a luxurious but business- like flat at 10 Hanover-square, London, W. A. "Weekly Dispatch" reporter found him arranging for the pro- duction of elle of la's plays at a London .theatre. " Unlike most playwrights," lae said, "I do not con- sider myself a genius with a mission to teach actors and educate the public taste. Neither do I seek introduction to any lessee, manager or actor.</p>
        <p>" When I first began I had one success and several failures, but I looked upon the latter as pointing the road to future successes. I never work on a play more than four hours a day, and I don't believe in night work. I can account for the partial failure of my play 'Oh! Angeline ' by the fact that at the time I was writing it I was in very bad health. "I was ill from the end of 1899 until the end of the summer, 1901, and during this period I consulted quite half a dczen doctors in America, England, and the Con- tinent, but nothing did roe any good until I beard of Dr William" pink pills for pale people. What were my Symptoms ? Well, I bad severe pains at the back of my neck, my breath was very short, whilst for days together I had most severe headachas. When going up- stair. I could !namely breathe. Different doctors attri- buted my sufferings to lumbago,to chest complaint,rheu matism,and heart disease. During my last voyage from , America' spoke to a friend,wbo toll me that if I bought just six boxes of Dr Williams' pink pills as soon as I landed I should be a new man before I bad finished them. He was right; my digestion and appetite improved. I grew strong and stout. Before my illness I weighed list. 13 Ih., whilst my ' best weigh during my ill-health was 9ot. 41b. Three days ago I turned the balance at flat. 71b. !" When oases so severe and complicated are cured, it is no wonder that minor ailments—indigestion, bile, rheu- matism, and bronchitig—yield to a few doses of the same pills ; while severs ailments, as paralysis, consumption, and kidney disease are cured by a little perseveranoe,as many published cases prove. On another page of this paper will be found an article on II eart Disease, and particulars of a case cured.</p>
        <p>A........,0_, , . ,„„,,,,,,,,,, ,•_ . ~,,,, ~.. i'll' lAft ,4 ---- - . _ Kr. L. Rodgers, the well-known Playwright. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>WARRINGTON UNION ASSESSMENT COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON UNION ASSESS- MENT COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this committee was held in the Board Room at the Workhouse, on Friday. Mr G Piling pre- sided and there were also present : Messrs William Houghton, A J Jolley, A Jolley, .1 Billings, J Clarke, W J C Sutton (clerk), William Owen (valuer), W H Wood- cock (assistant overseer for Warrington). THE SUPPLEMENTAL VALUATION LIST. A SMALL INCREASE. The ASS'S-RANT OVERSEER submitted the supplemental valuation list for the Borough of Warrington for the past six months. From this statement it appeared that in April last, the gross rateable value of the Borough was £296,244, agricultural laud £1,905 104, buildings £240,508. Iu November. the gross rateable value bad increased to £297,487, and agricultural land had de- creased to £1,873 and buildings had increased to £241, 71 10s. In answer to the CLERK, the ASSISTANT OVERSEER said the increase during the past twelve months was £3,000. The CLERK informed the Committee that the average increase in former Veir4 we about £5,000 per annum. The ASSISTANT OVERSEER said there bad not been any great increase since the revaluation was made. Mr A J JOLLEY : There has not been that room for it. The ASSISTANT OVERSEER : There has not been as much buildine going on intithe town.We are hoping for a much larger increase on the figures I have given in the ensuing twelve months. There are the tramways to be assessed, and we shall have an increase from the electricity undertaking. The CLERIC : There will also be Greening's works and extensions at other works to come in the next list. The list was then signed. TEE VALUER'S REPORT. This report referred to the following valuations and inspections :—ln the township of Warrington. Messrs Hamblen Bros, Turner-Street. New warehouse, stable and land, gross, £25, rateable, £2l. The Cheshire Lines Railway Committee. Siding to Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co. Ltd., to be raised from gross, £65, ratable £5O. to gross £95 10s. ratable, £B2 10e. The St Helens Cable Co. Ltd., Bank Quay. The new buildings are not completed, and no manufacturing is going on in them. Re Technical Schools. He(MrlOwen)was'asked to re- port on the assessment of Technical schools, seeing that the new building in Warrington would be occupied!by students within the next few weeks. He had prepared a table of the assessments fixed on such buildings in 9 towns. of which 6 were in Lancashire. The assessments might be called nominal except that in Bootle, which was about 3 per cent on the outlay. The discretion as to whether the assessment was to be nominal rested with the committee. The cost of the building in Was rington, with land, might be said to be about £15,000, and if the committee decided on a nominal assessments gross £2OO would meet the case. Mr A JOLLEY said it bad been stated by the Non- Trading Committee that the cod of the institute was £19,000. The VALUER said he bad been architect for the build- ing and ought to know what the cost really was. The report was adopted and a nominal assessment of £2OO gross for the Technical Institute.</p>
        <p>Patrick Leggett was hanged at Glasgow on Wednes- day for the murder of his wife. He said, before the crime, that be" meant to die on the scaffold." Lord Hawks, the captain of the Yorkshire Cricket Club, is suffering from a fractured conar-boae,sustained in the hunting field on Tuesday. On Wednesday the team of eTioireters, organised by Lord Hawks and captained by P F Warner, of Middle- sex, left Liverpool by the Majestic for Now York.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>A sermon to young men was preached in this church on Sunday evening by the Rev John Yonge. His sub- ject was" A Christian Hero and Martyr," and referred to the pioneer missionary in New Guinea, the Rev George Chalmers, who was massacred by the natives. In an interesting way,the preacher traced the life history of this notable missionary and paid a tribute to his ' steadfastness of purpose and devotion to duty, despite the dangers which daily beset him. In conclusion the preacher urged young men to be loyal to Christ as Chalmers was.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WYCLIFFE P.S.I. SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.I. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance at an " open meeting " of this society on Sunday when the members were per- mitted to bring their wives and lady frier:tole. Mr Councillor Brough presided and in the course of his address expressed his pleasure at seeing se many young people present. They had a clean page of life before them and he hoped they would see to it that it was not stained by sin. He appealed to them to honour their parents and not to be ashamed of them. Turning to the parents prosent, the Chairman asked them to so live as fathers and mothers that their chil- dren might respect and honour them.—Mr J L Fenn, of I Liverpool, based an address on the words " God has spoken." He pointed out that if people were dumb to God it was because they were deaf to Him. Miss Eckersley's fine contralto voice was heard to advantage in the solos " Jesu lover of my soul" and "Sun of my soul."—The Rev John Yonge stated that the member- / ship of the Society was steadily increasing and Wa3 no v over 500.—A collection was taken an bohalf of the funds of the Society.</p>
        <p>INDUCNON OF THE NEW VICAR OF sr JAMES',</p>
        <p>LATCEIFORD.</p>
        <p>The Rev F R Pearson (who has been privately in- stituted by the Bishop) was inducted to the living of St James', Latchford, on Saturday afternoon by the Von Archdeacon Barber. A fairly large congregation assembled. The usual form of induction was gone , through, Archdeacon Barber delivering a short address. Be said that the Bishop of the diocese was in theory the pastor of all the ©UN in that particular pert which they called the diocese. There were in the diocese van- ens parishes, and in each the Bishop entrusted to him who had been duly presented to him the duty of look- ing after the lambs and sheep of the fold. Ho dale. gated to him his soiritual authority, but until Mr Pearson, had been inducted he could take no legal action as vicar ot the parish. There had been many changes in the neighbourhood during the last four years, and there had been c'aangee of !Actin, but they ought to feel thankful that God pro- vided for the due carrying on of the work of His Church and for the supply of the means of grace to them. He continued to them, through the different channels, the same grace, the same sacraments, the same ministry of consolation and explanation of God's word, even though the channels might Be changed. A thirsty man who bad had a refreshing drink of water did not stop to consider how that water had been conveyed to him. He did not criticize, but was thankful for the refreshing draught, and thanked God for it. Therefore they, as a congrega- tion, should thank God that He gave to them this suc- cession of His ministers,this due supply ef grace through His sacraments; and if they did so they would te less nclined to criticize those who were the agents of bring- ing the supplies to them. -</p>
