      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Doctor's Fatal Mistake at Widnes.</head>
        <p>Doctor's Fatal Mistake at Widnes.</p>
        <p>At Widnes on Saturday a verdict or" Death from misadventure " was returned at an inquest held on the body of Loonar -1 Higgins,of 40, Gladston,-street,Widnes, aged seven rnooths, the child of Joseph Higgins. At the opening of the inquiry Mr Brighouse, the noronu, said it was a very sad case, and read a letter he had received from Dr Eatock, ef Widnes. This stated that Mrs Higgins took the child, who was suffering from teething troubles, to the doctor,and be prepared a bottle of medicine. At the same time there was lying on the surgery table an exactly similar bottle, which was nokbelled, and cant tined a solution of car- bolic acid in glycerine, of a strengt% of one part to five. When affixing the label the doctor rook up the carbolic solution by mistake, and discovered the error some twenty minutes later. Be rode at once to the mother's hOuse and found that ohs had given the child a doss of the poison. He administered antidotes and called in Dr Edwards, and together they did all they could until the child died. The Coroner pointed out that Dr Eatock stood well in his profusion as a careful practitioner, and it was an error such as any human being might make. The jury returned a verdict as above, adding that they agreed that blun-flated bottles or bottles of special form or colour should ho used for poisons. They attached no blame to the doctor.</p>
        <p>Dr P.)stook was present, but seemed very mucb upiPt. At the conclusion he attPmpted to express his sorrow, but broke down.</p>
