      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>-- MEETINGS OF THE GENERAL COMMITTER. DICKENSON'S SUSPENSION. A meeting of the General Committee of the Northern Union was held last night at the George Hotel, Hu ldersfield, Mr J Cliff oed (Huddersfield) presiding. The Committee tocepted the explanation of the Woolroyd Club with referenes to playing Feather, and requested that they do in future avoid the playing of professionals belong- ing to other clubs, without first getting permits from the club for which they are registered. The question of the mutual agreement of the Football Association recognising suspensions, .tc , was left over till the next meeting pending communication ith the Football Association. In answer to a communication from the Hull club, the committee decided that the Durham and Northumberland county were quite in order in claiming Thompson's services. The Committee decided to make a grant of £25 to the widow and family of the late J T Conley. of Huddersfield, the amount to be ptid over at the discretion of Mr Clifford. The following punishments were meted out for breaches of professional laws :—Millom fined one guinea for a breach of of professional law 1, sub-section B; Flimby fined 10s 6d, and H Graham suspended up to and inoluding January 1, for breaah of professional law 1. sub section C ; Hunslet fined one guinea, and J Jackson suspended up to May 1, for breach of professional law 2 ; G Roscoe, for breach of professional law 1, sub-section C, suspended to November 30. The explanation of the Neville Hornets Club. Oldham. was accepted. C H M'Cann, for breach of professional law 1, section 0, was suspended to May 1. The South Shields club was fined be 6d for a breach of professional law 3 ; Warrington were fined 10s 61, and G Dickenson was Sus- pended up to and including December 13 for a breach of professional law 1, sub-section C, second paragraph. The Committee severely censured Mr If S Thomas,secretary of High Moor Rovers Club, for a breach of professional law 1, sub-section 2. The case of Glover Nayloe, of Normanton, was held over till the next meeting, Naylor to be suspended pending such meeting. The following rulings were given by the committee on questions asked, as under : —By Mr Addison, of Fethergill ; question, ° Iles a partisan touch judge power to enter the field of play and repo-t to the referee rough or foul play and get a player sent off the field lt" Answer, He has power to report foul play, but not to demand the withdrawal of a player. Asked by Mr T A Rollin, Wakefield and District League: -(1) " If a player takes a free kick in front of the goal poets, places the 'Nall, and kicks it between the posts and over the cross_bar and the wind blows the tall back into the field of play with the result that a player running up catches the ball and grounds it over the goal line, should the referee allow a goal or a try r—The referee should allow a goal (2). If a player running with the ball gets to the goal-line, the ball is grounded on the goal-line, which should It be, a try or a five yards scrummage '—Answer : The correct decision, a try.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE,</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE,</p>
        <p>First Division.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON V OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>This popular fixture was played at Warrington on Saturday In windy, showery weather Notwithstanding the elements there would be quite 4,00) spectators, and the turf was In' good order. Owing to the suspension of Dickenson the pre- vious evening Warrington played Edmunds at centre, while Oldham had to substitute S Williams for James. Civil taking the centre position. Mr J Priestley was the referee and the teams lined out as follows Warrington—Knight, basic; Fish. Isherwood, Edmunds, and Harris, three-quarter backs ; Burgess and Hockenhall, half backs; Boardman, Roberts, Edmondson, F Swift, Forster, Morrison, W Swift, and Taylor. forwards. O'dham—R L Thomas, back; Spottiswood, P Thomas, Civil, and S Williams. three-quarter backs ; S Lees and A. Lees, half backs ; J H Wilkinson, 11 Wilkinson, Vowles, Owen, Ellis, Topham, Ferguson, and Fratsr. forwards, Taylor kicked off against the wind, and Boardman being I offside, the first scrimmage was formed at centre. The I Oldham forwards dribbled away past Edmunds and Hamm, but Knight nipped in finely and saved a dangerous rush. Mulls by the Warrington backs almost let Oldham in, but a fine kick saved Warrington for thelbsll went in touch near the quarter-flag. The game had opened very slowly, and a good prospect of passing was spoiled by Hockenhall holding too long. An attempt by Oldham was spoiled by Fish, who sprinted across the field, and easily beat the opposing basks, and licked in touch in a favourable position. Oldham attacked repeatedly BO far,but they showed none of their old-time play. Each team was frequently penalised for feet up, and there must have been a dozen penalties during as many minutes. The first round of passing took place between Burgess, Roakenhidi, and Edwards, but Civil, however, intercepted the lattees pass. Knight stomping the progress of the Old- ham centre. A bad kick by Edmunds almost led to Thomas breaking clear s way, but fortunately Harris just managed to hold him when he had no one else t ) fasts. Burgess and Hockenhall broke away. and the latter kicked across to Fish but Sam Williams just beat Fish for possession. From his kick the ball was allowed to roll dead by Knight. Arthur Lees got away splendidly from a scrimmage. but Hockenhall bowled him over before he got dangerous, and the next minute S Lees, jun., got a pass from Arthur Lees, and got a flue chance. Be hesitated how- ever, and thus lost a golden opportunity. finally passing forward to Ferguson. Play. however. continued of a most disappointing character, and many were the cries of " Play the game." At length a bit of exciting play occurred for Hoekenhall kicked across to Harris, and the latter easily beat Spotitiswood. With a good chance of a score Harris dashed up, but stumbled and fell, and was then easily caught. After a spell of mid-field play a big punt by Dick Thomas sent the ball dead. Oldham had the wind,but never looked like scoring, and a biager contrast to that whim, was played last year can scarcely be imagined. A sudden turn took oleos inn the game. for Fish intercepted a pass at the Warrington quarter-line, tied ran nearly the length of the field, He beat Thomas but Sam Williams caught him. No however, passed to Burgess. who had the misfortune to fail, and grounded the ball inside the field of play, and thus just missed a score. Shortly afterwards Isherwool made a mark before the post, but Fisk failed at goal. Oldham returned their attack after this, and though Ferguson had a kick at goal, they only got a barren minor. Near half-time, Sam Lees dropped a goal, and thus opened the scoring. The whistle blew for half-time a few minutes later, Oldham leading by one goal to ail. Frater restarted, and Warrington now having the wind behind them, at once settled in the Oldham half, and from the first scrum "a passing round between Burgess,Hocken- hall, Edwards, Isherwood and Fish ended in the latter racing round Thomas and planting the ball over the line. He only just missed a goal from a very difficult angle. This sensational score put the Warrington spectators in a better humour than had been the case since the commencement of the game, and was the outcome of the first really good round of passing. Oldham attacked for a time after this reverse, and Ferguson lad an unsuccessful shot at goal. A free kick at centre, given to Fish, was well judged, and the ball went in touch at the corner. Some stiff scrimmages followed on the Oldham line, bat the forwards broke away and carried the ball to the quarter line. Warrington had the majority of the play but the passing was frequently erratic, or there must have been more scoring. Final—Warrington 3 points, Oldham 2 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>RUNCORN v. :HALIFAX.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. :HALIFAX.</p>
        <p>--- - At Runcorn. Considerable interest was centered in the match and despite wet weather there was a good attendance Both teams were representative, Jolly again turning out for Runcorn.—Runcorn won the toss Little starting against, a stiff breeze. Jolly returned. A. brilliant run and kick by Price placed the visiters' line in 3eopardy,Little saving. Run- corn still pressed, a beautiful bit of combination giving the home team a geed opportunity Butterworth being collared In time. RURCOM still pr:ssed but little exciting play was witnessed. Jolly made a good opening but the ball was thrown forward to Jones. A moment later Honghton missed his kick Regg picking up and enabling Williams to scare between the posts Hadw.n failed to convert. Halifax con- tinues] to press Little finding touch In fine style repeatedly. Shortly after Little mulled, Runeora missing a splendid chance to score. The visitors still kept up a st übb.,)rn defence, the Linnets attacking strongly. Alter several serums Runcorn were awarded a penalty Jolly miEsin7, the sticks by inches. Alter this the visitors . got t zether splendidly,showing far superior combination to the Linnets. A free kick relieved but the Halifax front rank again got to work with one of their dangerous dribbles and were again within an ace of scoring. By easy stages the ball was worked away Richardson making a desperate attempt to sheer through his opponents. Little again mulled and gave the home team a chance. Houghton with a fine kiek Found touch well in the visitors (mart era Jolly being collared by Morley when he looked like scoring. Runcorn were not to be denied and after a particularly fierce attack the ball after being bundled by all the backs was grounded by Butter worth aver the line. Price tticked ti:r goal and struck the</p>
        <p>cress bar, the bi 11 rebounding back into play. Half-time arrived with each team claiming a try. The change of ends saw Runcorn open out the attack finely but a smart round of passing between Weigewood and Rigg gave the visitors considerable ground. Some tine kck- ing by Jolly again gave Runcorn the advantage Price placing the leather in beautiful style on the line The advantage, however, was lost through Butterworth kicking wildly. By a series of hard scrums Halifax worked the leather to the posts,where they were awarded a free kick, Little scoring a goal. The visitors still attacked keenly. Runcorn playing a magnificent defensive game. Hadwin bad a capital shot at goal, the leather just sailing outside. A break away, initiated by Richardson, took the leather to the Weston quarters, where Richardson was grassed almost on the line. From the subsequent scrum Dutton got over but , was brought back. The pressure was still keenly maintained I Richardson being held on the line. Eventually Riley tor the I visitors cleared. A brilliant bit of passing between Richard- son, Jolly and Price resulted in Jones losing at the critical moment. Shortly after a tine burst of passing resulted in the line being agaita crossed, but the referee again ordered I the ball baak. The Linnets were having by far the best of Use game and deserved to score but repeatedly fouled through sheer hard luck. In the latter stages of the game there was I only one team in it—and that was not Halifax Runcorn I could not, however, score, the game ending in a win for Halifax by one goal one try to a try.</p>
        <p>ST HELENS v LEICIII•</p>
        <p>Played at St Helens before 2,000 spectators. During the first half the Leigh men played with a stiff breeze at their banks, but had the beet of the game, which was. however, hard fought, Hinilon, the visitors' new half back, greatly distinguishing himself. There was no score up to the Interval. _ _ _</p>
        <p>After the change of ends the Leichites put on pressure, and the home forwanis were dangerous on more than one eocasien. Mercer made a mark, from which he kicked a goal. The remaining petiod was full of excitement. as Leigh quickly asserted their supremacy. A bad mull by Porter let in Roberts, who gained a try, and Johnson kicked a goal. Strong pressure by Leigh ended in Bennett scoring another try, and immediately afterwards passing by Realty, Davies. and Bennett finished with the last-named scoring a I try, and the same player kicked a goal. The result was a victory for Leigh by 2 goals and 3 tries (13 points), to 1 goal (2 points). Teams _</p>
        <p>et; Helens. —.Porter, full back ; J Creevey, C Oreevey. Dickenson. and Div:n.34lo, three-quarter backs ; Hail and Banks, half backs ; W Briers. Carney, Mooney, Whiteley, Thomson, Preseott, Green, and Mawson, forward. Leigh.—Pearson, full back; Gauley, Davies Johnson, and Neville, three-quarter backs ; H Bennett and Hinson, half backs ; Boardman, W Roberts. J Roberts, Arrowsmith, Kelly Web3ter. Jameson. and Kirk, forwards. . _</p>
        <p>Reteree, Mr J Bruckshaw. Stockport ; touch judges, Messrs EH smirk, Wigan, and W Nevins, Warrington. WIDNES v. DUNSLET.</p>
        <p>These teams met on the Parkaide Ground, at Hunslet, in the presence of about a thousand spectators. Hamlet opened the game. and for some time kept play in their opponents' half. At length Robinson got hold and passed to Walter Goldthorpe,who in turn threw to Whiteley. The latter ran strongly, but was pushed into touch at the corner. Soon afternards, however, Whiteley was given another chance, and this time he was successfal in planting the ball the line in the coruer. Albert Goldthorpe took the kick.but failed. After this the game fluctuated a good deal,and It was not until close upon half-time that any eventiworthy of comment happened. Then gthe ball got into the handsgof the Widnes backs, and Peacock making a fine spurt, managed to score under the posts. Tilley made the three points into five, so that at the interval Widnes were leading by a couple of points.</p>
        <p>As soon as the game was resumed, Hunslet tried hard to equalise. and in this they suceeeded,Albert Goldthorpe drop- ping a goal from a mark ny Whiteley. Although Runslet had the best of matters right up to the finish,they were unable te add to their vore.and the game ended in a draw—five points each,</p>
        <p>BRIGHOUSE V. Hutt. These teams met on the Lane Head ground, Brighouse, before about 1,000 spectators. Half-time score—Brighouse, 1 try (3 points) ; Hull, 1 try (3 points) From a kick by Cook, Saville ma le a mark sear the centre, and by a long raking shot the same player landed a goal. Result—Brig. house, 1 goal and 1 try (6 points) ; Hull, 1 try (3 points).</p>
        <p>WIGAN • Swcrrorr. _ These teams suet at Central Park, Wigan, before an attend- of some 10,00 d persons. In the first half the home - contingent faced a powerful sun, and for some time were forma to act on the defensive. Rothwell repeatedly cleared when the Wiganers were bard pressed,but a brilliant run by Wallwork had the effect of again endangering the home line ' the Swinton man being brought down near the posts. Strong forwards rushes on the part of the Wiganers marked the later stages of the game up to the interval. In the second half Wigan had the best of the contest, the Swinton players seldom getting over the half-way line. The visitors, however, offered a stout defence. Wigan had several shots at goal, but were unsuccessful, the encounter terminating In a draw, neither side having scored.</p>
        <p>BRAT,FoRD v In= KINGSTON ROVERS. Played on the Craven-street ground, Hull, before a fair attendance Starks won the toss, and Broadley kicked off against a strong wind. For some time play was pretty even. Each side had a penalty kick, but no score resulted. At length the Bradford backs got the ball and carried it along grandly, beating all opposition with tactful passing. Sinton got over the line, grounding the ball near the post, and Mosby added the goal. Almost immediately afterwards the I lovers dribbled the hall over the line from a scrummage near the Bradford " 25 "flag. and West scored, Starks kicking the goal, and thus equalising the game. At half-time each team had 5 pointe.—Atter the interval, Bradford soon gained the ascendancy. Dunn scored the neat try, and the goal was easily added by Mosby. Result :—Bradford, 3 goals and 3 tries (12 points) ; Rovers, 1 goal and 1 try (5 points).</p>
        <p>BROUGHTON BANGERS V. HUDDERSFIELD.</p>
        <p>Flayed at Wheaters Field, Broughton, before 4,0)3 people, A bout of passing by thc home backs saw Hammon pass to Wilson, who got over between the posts. W James took the place, but failed. By means of a forward rush Huddersfield reached the centre line, but the ball was soon returned, an Brouchton attacked warmly. Wilson being tackled by Kitchen within a few yards of the line. After repeated pressure. Widdeson ran over at the corner, from a pass by ti James the player last-named failing at goal. Half-time : Broughton Bangers 2 tries (6 points), Huddersfield nil. On resuming the Rangers assumed the aggressive, and R James gave to Wilson. who transferred to Hampson, and the last-named scored near the posts, S James's kick failed. Result :—Broughton Rangers 3 tries (9 points). Huddersfield nil.</p>
        <p>Every Woman is t . r,tterested and should know 'AI about the wonderful MARVEL WHIRLING SPRAY. Ask your Chemist for it. If he cannot supply "I / / the Marvel accept no other 9 'ft but send starnpfor book giving . iill iLll particulars and directions ' '';./ fa t invaluable to ladies. MARVEL CO, ' (Patented.) Dept. 143, 11, Queen Victoria Street, London, E.C.</p>
