      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>BATLEY T SALFORD.</head>
        <p>BATLEY T SALFORD.</p>
        <p>This match took plae3 at Batley. before a small ring of spectators. Three minutes from the start Davies score d a magnificent goal with the left foot, when hemmed In by several opponents. This put the visitors on their mettle,and the nest few minutes saw them in the Batley half. Batley after this took play near to the Salford use. Here Hodgson pieked up and passed to Spurr, whe transferred to Davies, and that player raced over at the corner. The goal kick failed. Result :—Batley 1 goal and I try (5 points), Salford 1 goal points). Teams :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A ' DEFEATED AT OLDEAM.</p>
        <p>Warrington "A" journeyed to Oldham on Saturday wits a very weak team and sustained a severe defeat. There wa- a moderate attendance of spectators. After a spell of for ward play Oldham scored through Cash,Wood failing at goal. Gardner soon added another try. Cox failing to improve the point 'Alen for a time the visitors showed up well and the home IA were kept on the defensive, but just before the interval Tyson scored from lirady's pass from the scrimmage. The second half was more tame and scoring was not so fast. It was evident tint the visitors were not equal to the Oldham team, and the home lot were rather inclined to rest content with their substantial lead. Waterhouse scored just before the finish. and Wormald kicked the only goal of the match. Final :—Oldham ('' A "). one goal four tries (14 points) ; Warrington (" A "), nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>WESLEY GUILD. —A lecture was given before the mem- bers of this euild on Monday evening by the Rev W Neville entitled " The Dairyman's Daughter in the Isle of Wight." It was illustratod by a number of pictures of lovely bits of Isle of Wight scenery, which were thrown on the screen by means of a limelight lantern.</p>
        <p>Spiritualist Society. A service of song, written by Mr C Llewellyn, entitled " Little Jess, the Messenger of Love," was creditably rendered in the Spiritualistfiall,Bewsey- street, on Sundayaevening. There was a good con- gregation. The story is simple and pathetic, and certain phases of spiritual phenomena are intro- duced. The hymns, selected from the Lyceum Manual, the Spiritualist hymn book, and other sources, were very suitabie, and as variety was given to them by being variously sung as solos, duets, trios,and choruses by the choir,they afforded considerable pleasure. Mr C Furness red,' the story and Mr Joel Foster, who had trained the choir, presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
