      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>NOVEMBIIIi 14th, 1902.</p>
        <p>NATURALLY Mr BALFOITIi Stifling Discussion of the Education Bill, exerted himself to the</p>
        <p>utmost limits of his powers in recommending to the House of Com- mons his formidable resolution "to expedite busi- ness on the Education Bill." He had to begin with no case; yet he was asking the House to adopt a course of procedure which could only find its justification, if at all, in such continuous and open obstruction of the will of the House as was resorted to by himself and his friends whilst he was still a meteoric sort of member of the Fourth Party. In such an emergency it is a good thing if one can fall back on authorities. Fully con- vinced on this point, the PRIME MINISTER now pays to Mr GLADSTONE'S memory a compliment such as he never paid that great statesman during his lifetime. What Mr GLADSTONE did is a model of constituticnal procedure for all time—such is the present doctrine of the Government front bench. The circumstances under which Mr GLADSTONE considered a distasteful line of con- duct to be unavoidable are, of course, sunk out of sight. It is conveniently presumed that his course of action would have been the same if the circumstances had been wholly different. The presumption is good enough for the moment, and for the purposes of the moment. Mr. BALFOUR never takes long views. He is content with just such arguments as he believes Al tide over an emergency. But if one thing can be much more certain than another it is that Mr. GLADSTONE, however important might have been the measure be had in hand, would never under similar con- ditions to those which now exist,have proposed such a resolution as that which Mr. BALFOUR gracefully and brilliantly, but also somewhat halt- ingly and shamefacedly, asked the House of Commons to accept on Tuesday. The PRImE MINISTER seems to have been fully conscious of the weakness of his position. He admitted that there has been no obstruction tu the Education Bill, but some of the speeches have seemed to him to be irrelevant. There have been ' more amendments than he can approve of. But there has been no deliberate waste of Par- liamentaryrtime. There has been no debating for debate's sake. There have been no speeches the purpose of which was to prevent a proper dis- cussion of the Government measure. Every con- dition existing when Mr. GLADSTONE resorted to closure by compartment on the Home Rule Bill has been positively and absolutely reversed.. AU that Mr. BALFOUR can say in favour of his revolu- tionary proposal is that, although the discussion has been perfectly legitimate, it has taken .along time and is likely to take a much longer time if the House does not consent to apply the shears. It was a wretched case stated with great charm of manner, with sudden spurts of candour, and at the</p>
        <p>same time that he is t amounts I discussions be violent cause the "We cam BALFOUR, think must on both I about SOME The fact is</p>
        <p>HENRY CA</p>
        <p>out, he was that " the debate on 1 to degradl machine, tu this House policy by a and injuric for the cou resolution motives of was arbitrs highest nal same effec saying an decidedly f</p>
