      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <p>same time with the confidence of one who knows that he is the master of legions. Summed up, it amounts to the bald statement that the discussions of the Education Bill must be violently and senselessly curtailed be- cause the Christmas holidays are approaching. "We cannot go on interminably," says Mr. BALFOUR, an argument which he appears to think must be absolutely convihcing to members on both sides. He has modified his opinions about some things, he told the House yesterday. The fact is indisputable, for nine years ago, as Sir HENRY CAMPBRLL-BANNERAIAN was able to point out, he was supporting a resolution which declared that "the proposal of the Government to curtail debate on the Irish Government Bill is calculated to degrade the House of Commons to a voting machine, and to deprive the British majority in this House of its constitutional right to discuss a policy by which British interests will be seriously and injuriously affected." There was no necessity for the course proposed by the Government, the resolution went on to declare; it was dictated by motives of party expediency. The Government was arbitrarily limiting debate on a measure of the highest national importance—with more to the same effect. This was what Mr. BALFOUR was saying and supporting nine years since. Most decidedly he has " modified his opinions." The Home Rule Bill had a strong public mand- ate behind it. Its policy had received the endorse- ment of the constituencies at a general election; it was supported by a considerable majority in the House; but the proper debating of its provisions was impeded by the wild and intemperate Tory opposition. Said Mr. CHAMBERLAIN, supported and cheered by Mr. BALFOUR, the proposal to apply the guillotine was"the argument of tyrants" and acquiescence in it would be "the creed of slaves." The Government would be gagging the opponents it was unable to answer. How exceed- ingly applicable are these words to the situation of to-day! But how wholly and startlingly different is the position of the Government. There is no general endorsement of the Education Bill. That measure has been repudiated by large meet- ings all over the country. Many of the members on the Government 'benches are supporting it reluctantly and mutinously at the bidding of the party Whips. It has not been obstructed, but merely debated. Nevertheless "the argument of tyrants" is to be applied; the "creed of slaves is to be accepted; the Government is to " gag the opponents it cannot answer " ; and all because Christmas will be here in a few weeks' time. Was there ever such a Robelaisian political situation before ?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>imm...,..,...,,......w..........1.•</head>
        <p>imm...,..,...,,......w..........1.•</p>
        <p>Probably no one in Warrington has taken a deeper interest in its ancient history than Mr. Thomas May. Undeterred by the scepticism with which his discoveries were at first received, Mr. May has, with unremitting zeal, devoted himself to the task of bringing to light antiquarian treasures which form a link with the tinct when the Romans held sway in the land. His excava- tions at Wilderspool have established beyond a doubt the fact that a Roman town existed there, and some of the remains go to prove con- clusively that Wilderspool, in those early days, was an industrial centre. List week, the members of the Warrington Society, under the guidance of Mr.. May, spent" A night in Roman Warrington." In other words, they had the opportunity ot listening to a highly interesting and instructive lecture by Mr May on thesubject which he has so much at heart. With thepride of the true enthusiast Mr. May gave exhaustive descriptions of the operations at Wilderspool, and of the fine collection of relics which he has unearthed. He was able to produce ample evidence to show that during the time of the Roman occupation, iron smelting was carried on at Wilderspool, and it is singular that at the present day the industry which affords employ. nient for so many inhabitants of the town is the iron trade. Warrington certainly owes a debt of gratitude to Mr. May for the researches which have revealed so much of interest to the towns- people generally, and to antiquarians in particu- lar.</p>
        <p>One of the London evening papers has been endeavouring to give its readers, on a scientific basis, a forecast of the kind of weather to be ex - perienced during the winter, but it must be ad- I mitted the result is not convincing. In the first place, there is some attempt to set up a law of probability, drawn from the experienee of recent ,years. OE the last seven winters, five have cer- tainly been mild, while the remaining two, includ- ing the last, have been by no means severe. The idea is rather a taking one, especially as last winter, with its one cold snap, certainly suggests, on retrospection, the turning tide. It is admitted, however, that this sort of con- sideration is terribly unscientific, and that a succession of mild winters no more makes a cold winter likely than a succession of sixes thrown at dice makes that number, or any other, more or less likely on the next cast. More weight can be attached to the fact that cold summers, such as that we have passed through, are oftener than not followed by cold winters. An examina- tion of sixteen instances of summers much below the average in the last sixty years shews that in eleven cases severe winters followed. It is evident, however, that there are numerous exceptions to this rule, and in four cases the discrepancy is very marked, cool summers being followed by winters of singular mildness. Our contemporary declares, with some hesitation, for a cold winter, but we must admit its reasons seem to beef the scantiest. Perhaps it is as well for weather prophets not to tender any explanation of their predictions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>11 Si. ASHTON'S LIFI4 TONIC IS THE KING! 0? TONICS —33, SINKET-SHZEEr, Warrington.—(Advt.) Association Football.</head>
        <p>11 Si. ASHTON'S LIFI4 TONIC IS THE KING! 0? TONICS —33, SINKET-SHZEEr, Warrington.—(Advt.) Association Football.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE LEAGUE. EARLESTOWN V BARROW. These teams met at Bylestown on Saturday afternoon 3Pfore a fair number of Condition'The ground was in a rauddy and slippery militated against good play, Another change was made in the playing full back vice Jones and Heardstthossemwte going , betweenLimon ci play up!hill with the wind slightly the sticks ia place of H °bin. Winning being favour in the visitors immediately attacked, ley inn wn raced n&amp;ao ailed chose to and they upon to save from Jackson. The ho Barlow who had an excellent opportunity to score y and uAnf or- tunately handled the ball and a free kick resulted. Barrow were played on the defensive.but Whitehouse stafiTield S gOod opportunity by infringing the offside rule. Some good play between Barlow and Stanton resulted In the former having an open goal,but at the critical moment he slipped and spoiled what was otherwise an excellent opportunity of opening the scoring. Later on Stanton shot past Stewart, but the whistle had sounded for offside. Play ruled fairly even, and the teams appeared to be pretty equally matched. Clever work by Whitehouse forced Marrion to concede a corner, but the resultant effort was safely negotiated. A stinging shot by Stanton missed the opening by Inches only. Barrow now had a chance and a good effort from the left wing was diverted by Beardshaw, but White- house meeting the ball banged it into the net. Immediately afterwards the interval was reached, the score standing. Barrow one, Earleetown nil. On re-starting EarlestoWn at once became dangerous, but they displayed extraurdhaary weakness in front of goal. The pace was fairly fast, and Barrow were unable to make any headway, the ball being continually in their quarters.btill Rarlestown with numerous openings were unable to equalise and the final score stood as at the interval.</p>
        <p>SPECIAL VISIT TO CREWE,—Yir Thomas Ison, Oculist and Aurist (ni Ison's Eye and Ear Dispensary, Leeds, established 1871). will visit the Royal Rotel, Crewe, on Wednesday, November 3.9tb, hours 11 to 1, and may be consulted on all cases of failing sight and hearing. New treatment without operation. Sufferers should avail themselves of the present opportunity of consultation. Spectacles adapted for week or defective eight. Those unable to avail themselves of a personal consultation, Should write to Leeds for printed form of questions for- warded free.—Letters to be addressed to 71, GREAT GEORGE-STREET, Leeds.—Advt.</p>
        <p>LAW APPOINTMENT. The Lord Chancellor has been pleased to appoint' as the Commissioner for oaths Mr II N Linaker of the firm Linaker and Linaker, High-street. FOREIGN BIBLE SOCIETY. The local branch of the above society was held on Tuesday under the presidency of the Rev J Osborne. The Rev J Monk-Jones delivered a very interesting lecture on" The Bible Society's work in Fiji." It was reported that over .£BO bad been sent to the parent Society. The customary vote of thanks concluded the meeting. • A BOATMAN DROWNED. Mr Dobson, coroner, held an inquest at the Run- corn Police Station on Monday relative to the death of Thomas Helps (26) a boatman whose body was found in the Bridgewater Canal on Sunday morn- ling. The evidence went to show that deceased had in all probability missed his footing in stepping from another boat to his own, on the night of the previous Monday when it was very dark and rain- ng.—A verdict of" Found drowned" was returned. OARSMEN DANCING. Last Friday the annual dance promoted by the Runcorn Boating Club took place in the Guild Hall, The hall had been tastefully laid out as a drawing- room, under the superintendence of Mr Hulse, the club's secretary. The dancing commenced at eight o'clock,and was kept up with zest until four o'clock the next morning. Limelight was used for the shadow dancing, There was an attendance of 86 Messrs R P Johnson and F G Chrimes officiated as M C's and the music was provided by Mr Tonks' istring band. The caterer was Mr F Walsh. RUNCORN SONS OF TEMPERANCE. Mr T Clarke presided at a meeting of the above I order held on Saturday evening. An interesting address was given by Mr Thomas J Holt on" Mode- ration." After showing the value of moderation in jeveryday life, he pointed out the great difficulty of 'carrying it out in regard to the use of alcoholic drinks. He spoke of the power of example, and strongly advocated teetotalism as the remedy for intemperance. On the motion of Mr Bisbrown, the discussion on the subject was adjourned until the , next meeting.</p>
        <p>HALTON CHURCH JUBILEE. The jubilee of Halton Parish Church has been celebrated this week. On Tuesday evening a parochial tea party was held. There were more than 100 present, and the tables were presided over by lady members of the congregation. Subse- quently a lantern lecture on " The history of the Church of England during the last fifty years "' was delivered by the Rev S Wilkinson, vicar of Timper- ley. The lanternist was the Vicar (the Rev G D Wray),—On the motion of Mr Hancock, seconded by Mr Meredith, a vote of thanks was accorded to Mr Wilkinson.—Mr Hancock mentioned that it bad been decided to erect a new window in the church in commemoration of the jubilee.—The room was afterwards cleared for dancing. and this completed the evening's entertainment. ""' Y" BRANCH OF RUNCORN WOMEN'S TEMPER- I ANON ASSOCHATIOM• I The monthly meeting of the above was held in the Ashridge-street Mission Hall on Wednesday' Mrs H Baker presided over a very fair attendance, and in her address gave some very interesting yet startling instances of the effects of intemperance MrsLeftwich of Liscard(President of the Lancashire and Cheshire Union of "Y" Branches) then ad- dressed the meeting and during the course of her remarks put the fact plainly that nothing is done without effort,as this alone leads on to success. She then went on to prove the physical, moral and spiritual advantages of total abstinence. The musical part of the programme was contributad by the following members :—Mrs Pritchard and the Misses Harrop, Stubbs, and Bell. Miss Clayden kindly gave a recitation. The singing of the Tem- perance Doxology brought the meeting to a close. DINNER TO RUNCORN COUNCIL'S EMPLOYEES. in connection with the Jubilee celebrations a dinner was given to the employees of the Runcorn Urban District Council on Friday evening last by MrSamuel Taylor, Chairman of the Council, in the small Public Hall. The dinner was supplied by Mr James Lea. There were over sixty employees pre- sent. In the unavoidable absence of Mr Taylor, Mr Handley presided. After the men had partaken of dinner the following toasts were given "The King," proposed by the Chairman. The next toast was "The Host, Mr Taylor," proposed by Mr Mandley and seconded by Mr Wilding. This toast was received most heartily. The Toast of the Council " was proposed by Mr Marshall, and Mr Handley responded. Interspersed were the following songs and recitations :—Mr Branch, songs, " Father O'Flynn," "Off to Phila- delphia," Boatswain's Story," " Admiral Tom ; Mr F Ellison, recitation, "Fagan on the Field" ; songs (humorous), "Come in," '' Sorry I called to-day," No time to tarry;" Mr R Morris, songs, " Old Folks at home," " Uncle Jack," " Judge's Song" (from "Trial by Jury.")—Ur Walter Jones proposed a vote or thanks to Mr Branch, Mr Morris. Mr Ellison, and Mr Grounds (pianist.) The pro- ceedings closed with the National Anthem.</p>
        <p>Readers of our advertisement columns will see that a public meeting to protest against the Educa- tion Bill will be held on Tuesday evening in the Greenway-road school. The member for the North- wich division with Henry Broadhurst Esq , are to be the chief speakers. There can be no doubt but there will be an exceptionally large attendance to pretest against a measure which strikes such a deadly blow at the liberties of the citizen. It behoves all Nonconformists, Educationists and Liberals to attead the meeting and show Sir J T Brunner that there is at least one division in the Country dead against the re-imposition of a Church rate.</p>
      </div>
