      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <p>FOUND DEAD AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The body of a man, apparently about forty-six years of age, was found on the waste tip, Moor- lane, Widnes, on Thursday morning. The police were notified, and the body was taken to the mortu- ary, there to await identification. Deceased belonged to the labouring class.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRI') AV: NOVEMBER</p>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLES TOWN. -- LBY A CORRESPONDBISTi. The Trades Defence Association has not extended its ramifications to Earlestown as yet, at least so tar as any active operations are concerned. The Co-operative Society, however, according to their report which they have issued to the members for the past quarter, shows that they do not intend to be caught napping. In the agenda is a recom- mendation to vote a guarantee of £lO to the Co- operative defence fund. This is a small amount but it shows that Co-operators as a whole intend to meet the private traders in any action they may purpose taking.</p>
        <p>The Conservative Association had their quarterly meeting on Saturday at which Lord Newton was present. There were not any momentous questions, other than the Education Bill, requiring the atten- tion of the meeting. Mr Sam Woods' supporters are working quietly, but nevertheless effectually, and this fact no doubt will tend to warrant the Conservatives seeing that their house is in perfect order. Politics in tne district are eta discount but it is likely that a large meeting in support of Mr Woods will shortly be held.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>POOLVID MYRIORAMA.</head>
        <p>POOLVID MYRIORAMA.</p>
        <p>The well-known entertainers, Messrs H snd Poole, commence: what promises to be a most suc- cessful twelve nights' engagement at the Runcorn Public Hall on Monday evening. This is not their first visit to Runcorn, and having regard to the all- round excellence of the entertainment which they always provide, it will be surprising if there are not large audiences.. In point of variety the entertainment can not possibly be beaten, and we can assure those of our readers who have not yet seen Poole's myriorama that they have a great treat in store. The pictures are both interesting', instructive and entertaining. In addition to the dioramic pictures there are shown animated photo- graphs, amongst which are the Preston Guild Pro- cession, the Coronation procession, the Coronation procession in Westminster Abbey, and also a num- ber of humorous pictures. The pictures are inter- spersed with musical selections and other varieties. The Cameo Troupe give a clever acrobatic perform- ance, and Mdlla Lizette and Master Charles are responsible for a marvellous exhibition on the tele- phone wire. Stewart Braids is well described as Dan Leno's double. The three Ogdens, mirth-pro- voking comedians, Condos and Una, Japanese feet equilibrists, Miss Marion Hemming, a charming soprano vocalist, and Coma, a Japanese lightning juggler, will contribute t 3 the evenings' entertain- ment, and we advise all our readers not to miss perhaps the best show of the season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY 'A CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>There has been absolutely nothing this week to ruffle the monotony of the commooplacelßuncorn like the majority of places during this season of the year, has lapsed into a comatose condition, out of which she will not be awakened until Father Christmas appears on the scene with his frost and snow</p>
        <p>The morale of the town is improving. If crime continues to decrease there will soon be no need for policemen, while the court-house will be regar- ded as a relic of ancient days.</p>
        <p>The Court officials, however, really need not des- pair. If there be men strong enough to refuse the "New Church Rate" there will soon be a glut of basiness--perhaps more than they want—if only making orders of distraint and commitment. Cheer up, you unworked Court officials, there's a better time coming!</p>
        <p>It is lamentable:to note, respecting the E -luna- tion Bill,that Runcorn has not yet awakened to the fact that the last ditch is being crossed. The gag and the closure—not the will of the people— will soon make the measure a "law of the land." Educationists, Liberals,Nonconfor mists,all lovers of the cowman weal will then have to decide whether they will still oppose the measure or whether they will place their necks under the clerical heel. No one perhaps will be disposed to blame the Educa- tionist and the Libetal who view matters from a purely political standpoint if they should meekly accept this—paradoxical as it may seem—Tory re- volutionary measure,but the Nonconformist—with a knowledge of the traditions of Nonconformity—will have some little difficulty to justify his conduct for refusing to fight the duel to the death. If the Dissenters of Runcorn, after all their mouthings- at the last I School Board election, allow them- selves, through a bastard reverence, to be forced into passive acquiescence with the rate. then the sooner the state Church swallows up Dissent in all its forms the better i will be for the sake of har- mony and Christianity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>The game at Runcorn on Saturday when Halifax were the visitors will long be remembered in the annuals of the Linnets. It was one of the most vexing, heartrending games, from a player's as well as from a spectator's point of view, that could be possibly witnessed. his was due to the sheer in- competence of the man who should be, of all men, the most expert on the field of play. I refer directly to the referee. From the outset, it was apparent he was either flustered or bothered by the points of the match and as the game proceeded it was "confusion worse confounded" with a venge- ance. In the early stages of the first half, Halifax had slightly the best of matters though Runcorn were playing with the wind. But the defence was keen and a score was happily averted. The visit- ors, however, managed to draw first blood, though the manner in which it was ;effected could hardly rank as first class football. Prior to the ball be- ing grounded the leather was not only knocked on by Houghton but -also thrown forward, yet no whistle went and the score was allowed. This reverse startled the home team,who from that time to the end of the match forced the game at a rapid rate and easily maintained the upper hand. The go. al—a penalty one—which gave Halifax the win- ning points was presumably given for feet up right under the home posts. The referee may have been quite right in penalizing for alleged " feet up "but the award of a free kicic under the posts certainly appeared to be severe in the extreme where there was an element of doubt. The first brilliant bit of play was witnessed when! Butterworth, sheering right through his opponents, scored a magnificent try. Runcorn still maintained the pressure and on two occasions the leather was grassed distinctly over the line and on each occasion it was brought back. Both times it appeared that the referee had committed a grave error of judgment,the most pro- nounced of the two being when a Runcorn player I actually grounded the ball, and a Halifax player running up kicked it into touch. The result- of the referee's mistakes—to put the matter mildly—was to lose the match. Naturally enough the spectators felt sore about the matter and they acted rashly and unwisely, though with some excuse, in retaliation. The referee was hooted and hissed as he passed from the field to the dressing room and a desperate attempt was made by a few outsiders to moblhim. Luckily he came to no harm. But a huge crowd of excited individuals surrounded the field and waited for his appearance. The referee wisely thinking discretion the better part of valour kept to his tent and refused to be drawn. Eventually after a weary waiting in company with one or two stalwarts a detour was made and the poor referee reached the station in safety. The mere fact that never in the history of the club has such a scene been witnessed testifies that the endurance of the spectators had passed the breaking point. In the interest of the Northern Union in general and the clubs in particu- lar such an incompetent should be removed from professional duty.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOITN WIDOW'S DEA.TII FROM BLOOD POISONING.</p>
        <p>Mr S Brighouse the County Coroner held an in- I quiry at the Town Hall, Earlestown, yesterday (Thursday),touching the death of Mary Ann Water- man a widow aged 62,and living at Chandos-street, Earlest wn, who died on Tuesday from the ,effects of an injury received whilst hanging a piece of meat on an hook in the ceiling. Mary Ann Broughton deceased's step daughter stated that on Friday October 24th her mother was hanging a piece of Meat on a hook in the kitchen ceiling. She stood on a stool and through some cause she slipped, the hook catching her fore-finger and tearing it severely. Witness bandaged it up but it became very painful and she went to the chemist who gave her some powder to put on. There was no improvement and she called in Dr Patterson, who dressed the wound. Fie stated that the powder that had been put on previously was not the right treatment and that there were traces of blood poisoning, Deceased gradually grew worse, and eventually died on Tuesday last. The cause of death was certi- fied as mortification. The Coroner read a letter from Dr Gangs, who had attended her prior to her death. It stated that when he examined deceased the hand was full of pus,—The jury, alter a short deliberation, returned a verdict of " Accidental death."</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Edwar Ilion presided over the monthly meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council which was held in Cairo-street Schools on Tuesday evening. The Secretary (Mr F Stott) was unable to be present, audit was decided that Mr Buckley should undertake the secretarial duties for ,the evening. . MR. BUCKLEY AND THE COUNCIL MINUTES. Mr. BUCKLEY moved that the Secretary be instructed to forward copies of the minutes of each Council meet- ing to affiliated and unaffiliated societies within a week of the meeting. He said he had strongly impressed the matter upon the Council before. The real objection to the proposal was the question of expenditure, but since he introduced the resolution, the balance sheet had been submitted, and there was a small amount in hand. Mr WYCH seconded. Mr &amp;MALEY opposed the preposition as he considered there was no necessity for the sending out of copies of the minutes, and it would entail extra work on the Secretary, and increased expenditure. After some discussion Mr Buckley expressed his willingness for a six months' trial of the scheme. Mr FLINSTON said that had it not been for the in- creased rental which the Council was paying he would have supported the proposal. Ho thought they ought to be certain of their income before they incurred additional expense. Mr BIICICLEY considered the t the increased rental was more fruitless than the expenditum on the minutes would be. He considered that the room was not suitable and the expenditure too large. On a vote being taken the resolution was defeated. THE PLACE OF MEETING. The Bakers' Society had forwarded a letter protesting against the amount which was being paid for the use of the room in Cairo-street schools for Council meetings (4s per night). It will be remembered that at the last meeting it was decided that the room be engaged for six months, and Mr W H Woodcock, treasurer of the schools, wrote to the Secretary: agreeing to this, with the provision of a month's notice on either side. Mr GREALET, the representative of the Bakers' Society, strongly objected to the arrangement, and said that the Society would rather leave the Council than that such an amount should he expended in rent. Ile moved that a mouth' e notice be given. Considerable disoussion took place, Mr FODMORE pointing out that the dignity of the Council had to be considered, aud he objected to meetings being held at public-houses. He moved that the Council adhere to the arrangement with regard to Cairo-street schools. Mr JONES seconded. The CHAIRMAN agreed with Mr Podmore and added that at some future time the Council would have to discuss the question as to whether a Trades Hall should be built in Warrington. If one were built each society and the members of different branches could take up shares. It would be a most suitable thing for the workingmen of Warrington to have such a hall. Mr GRELLEY caused some amusement by remarking that ho was agreeable to anything—even to meeting under a hedge—so long as the Council left that room. Mr Methem moved that a committee be appointed to make further inquiries and submit a list of not less than six available places at the next meeting. Mr EATON seconded. After further discussion Mr Malone' e proposition was carried, and it was agreed that Messrs Edwardson, Malone, and Stott should constitute the committee. OLD AGE PENSIONS. It was decided that Mr PMmb should attend a confer- ence of Trade Unionists, Co-operati:e and Friendly Societies, to be held at Manchester on January 17th, 1903, to discuss the question of old age pensions. IRONFOUNDERS BECOME AFFILIATED. A letter had been received intimating that the Warring ton branch of the Friendly Society of Ironfounders of England and Wales bad decided to become affiliated with the Council. SHOULD WARRINGTON HAVE A TRADES HALL? The CHAIRMAN again referrel to this matter and pointed out that there were buildings of this description at Halifax, Bolton, Heywood and various towns in Lan- cashire. He thought that considering Warrineton's large industrial population there ought to be a Trades Hall in this town. Of course it would be for them to decide whether the Trade Unioniste should co-operate with the Friendly Seeietyiesor work the scheme indepen- dently. The hall would have rooms in whichthe various branches could meet,and there would be no reed to ,eo to publichouses to hold meetings. He thought that ill the matter was properly taken up it would be brought to a successful issue. Mr PODMORE quite agreed that there was a necessity Ifor a hall. Undoubtedly these halls were very sinus s- lut in many towns, particularly in Lancashire. It was not, however, a matter for discussion that night, because they were not prepared to advance any scheme. What they wanted to do was to obtain some facts as to the working for and ultimate achievement of trades halls in other towns. If they could obtain that informa- tion together With an estimate of the cost and a plan, the delegates would be able M take the matter before the branches and have it thoroughly thrashed out. He moved that the Secretary be instructed to obtain infor- mation in reference to the balls in other towns and sub- mit it to the Council. Mr PLUMB seconded the motion, which was agreed to. THE OPENING OF THE TECHNICAL SCHOOL. " A VERY MEAN BUSINESS." The CIAIRm/IN informed the Council that an invitation was received for a representative of the Council to be present at the opening of the new Technical Schaol, and he attended the ceremony. Mr MALONE called attention to the fact that only one invitation was Sent Mr BUCKLEY considered it a very mean business that only one invitation should be sent. He moved that the Council protest against the action of the Museum Committee in not sending more invitations, as he con- sidered that the Council was fully entitled to them. There was no seconder to the proposition. Mr Podmere said that invitations seemed to have been sent to various organisations, He thought that considering the Council represented the great bulk of the working classes in Warrington, more invitations should have been sent. If they has a Council had not received more he considered that every trade unirm branch should have had one. If such invitetions had been issued it Would have incre seed the interest of toe working classes in the school. The matter then dropped and the meeting concluded.</p>
        <p>(\JURE PILE WIRE gives valet in one night. Are unequalled throughout the world. csitive Hundreds of testim onkel*. Of all Chemists, or i 1 (n Oliarr Exit Go ,27, giboe-lane, Leiden, E.O. (2)</p>
        <p>A very interesting ceremony in the shape of the open- ing of the new church parlour in connection with the above, took place in the lecture hall on Wednesday evening. The parlour has been constructed and decor ated by Mr Else, of St Helens, and adjoias the lecture , hall, the room being intended as a recreation room for the members of the church during the winter evenings. I It is beautifully deaorated and furnished and altogether provides a very enticing place of resort,especially for the young people connected with the church. The lecture hall was crowded at the opening ceremony, the Rev C E Dove occupying the chair. After Mrs C Hip wood had sung " The Promise of Life " the Chairman said that he hardly knew what to call the room. Some would like it termed " Toe Girls' parlour " whilst others thougut , " The Church Parlour" was a better designa- tion. However whichever name it was known by, he earnestly hoped that the room would be frequently used by all. Of course the ladies had been the chief movers in this affair, and for such they , deserved great credit. They had done all they possibly could to please, and to serve the interests of the Church. They could not please everybody, and he knew that someone would be diasatiafied with the colouring of the walls and the general looks of the room, but he hoped they would be as gentle in their eriticism as possible.— Mrs A Jones and Sister Adele, both of St. Helens, after- wards related their experiences in connection with Church parlours, the former in St. Helens and the latter in the Rest end of London. During an inter's-it Miss Olive Roberts displayed a voice of wide range in a Capital rendering of " For all Eternity."—The Parlour was afterwards formally opened by Mrs. Else, of St. Helens, the door being unlocked with a silver key pre- sented by Mrs Atkinson. 41•••••••••••••••••••••</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Earlestown greatly disappointed their supporters on Saturday when they allowed Barrow to snatch a couple of points at their expense. It was evident from the kick off that the home elevens were equal in merit if not better than their opponents, when playing in mid-field, but when near the goal they played no better than a batch of schoolboys. On the other hand Barrow were always dangerous and did their best to find the entrance to the net and it was only the sterling play of Beardshaw which prevented the score being heavier than it was.</p>
        <p>Brown was certainly not a success at centre for- ward, and it dld not improve matters when he changed places with Whitehouse. They bad many 1 chances of scoring but they appeared unable to avail themselves of the opportunities which came in their way. On Saturday Barlestown play Chorley on the latter's ground, and the opinion of the cora- -1 mittee is expressed in the change they have made in the team. The defence remains the samo, bw, in the forward line Brown partners Stanton, whilst Bartley (who played last season on the wing) goes centre forward, and Barlow assists Leach. This necessitates the dropping of Settle,in my opinion a mistake, but the result on Saturday will show the wisdom or otherwise of the change.</p>
        <p>What Newton's position in the Combination table will be, I cannot say, but unless they try to win a few matches, they will qualify for the wooden spoon. On Saturday they encountered White Star Wanderers on the latter's ground, and although they played a fairly goad game they were unable to prevail, and had to retire defeated by 3 goals to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>WARRINGTON TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL. Suggested Trades Hall.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL. Suggested Trades Hall.</p>
        <p>, 1902</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>NEWTON PARLIAMENTARY DIVISION. Mr. Sam Woods' Candidature.</head>
        <p>NEWTON PARLIAMENTARY DIVISION. Mr. Sam Woods' Candidature.</p>
        <p>' Oa Tuesday evening Mr Sam Woods, speaking at a meeting at Mirage, near Wigan, said he was coming 3ut as a Labour candidate for the Newton Parliamentary Division by authority of the Miners' Federation of Great Britain. He ventured to say that there never was an organisation that had done so much for protect- ing wages, in getting good laws passeel, and in preserv- ing life, as that Federation. He was not coming out in a new capacity. He had been in Parliament seven or eight years, and he hoped he had yielded some benefit to the working men of his country. it f orrieg to rise Workmen's Comp msation Act, he seid be was in the House of Commons when that bill was passel. He spoke on the second reading, and moved tnree important amendments. Colonel ell kiegton, tie present member for the div'sion, had the audacity to say that he was as much a Labour candidate as him (Mr Woods), who had spent thirty years of his life in the closest connection %vitt' Labour. The Executive of their Federation held a meeting in London a fortnight , ago, and decided that, in consegeence of the improve- ment of trade and the general prosperity of industries, they were entitled to an advance in wages of 10 per cent which the emp!oyers took off at the middle of the pee- smt year. They had evidence, however, already that the employers bad decided to ask for a five per cent reduction. He asked them to get in haruess, es an election might be pressed on them in a short time, and he believed that when the day ot the election came they would win the Newton Division. (Applause). The following resolution was unanimously carried : " That this meeting, having heirl Mr Sam Weeds, Labour candidate for the Newton-in-Makertield Parlia- mentary Division of the County of Lancaster, considers that he is a suitable person to reoreslnt the division in the Reuse of Commons, and pledges itself by all legiti- mate means to support him."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETIES AND PRIVATE TRADERS. The Establishment of a Trader's Union.</head>
        <p>CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETIES AND PRIVATE TRADERS. The Establishment of a Trader's Union.</p>
        <p>The movement on the part of tradesmen recently started at St Helens, and extended to Wigan Leeds, and other towns, has now become generil in the North of England, and it appears that Manchester is likely to be the centre of a readers Union for the protection of the interests of private traders in regard to co-operation. , The Co-operative Union haviag decided to raise a de- fence fund of £lOO,OOO for the protection of the interests of co-operators, something of a similar kind is to taken on the part of the traders, and a series of meet- ings had been arranged for the purpose of stating the case for the private trader as compared with that of the co-operator. It is theught that undue importance is given by public men to the work of those co-operative societies which are di•tributive.</p>
        <p>lane offices of the new Traders' Union are at 38, Corn Exchange Chambers, Fennel-street, and Mr John Williams (chairman of the Manchester, Salford, and District Grows' Association) is the president and Mr James Kendall the secretary. As a preliminary step the Union bas convened a conference of traders and manufacturers from all parts of the country to be held ,in Mancheiter on November 18h and 19th. The pro- ceedings will probably be private.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN CHURCH.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN 8r NEWTON.</p>
        <p>P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly meeting of the P S A was held in the Co-operative Hall, Earle-street, on Sunday. Mr J Smith presided over a large attendance. A short address was given by Mr G S King. Recita- tions were contributed by Miss Roberts and solos were rendered by air W Brown. Miss Williamson presided at the piano.</p>
        <p>TRAMCAR ACCIDENT AT HA.YDOCK. Whilst a tramcar was proceeding down Church- road, Ilaydock, between 4 and 5 p m, on Saturday last, owing to some misunderstanding the car col- lided with a trap owned and occupied by Mr Broadbent, with the result that Mr Broadbent was thrown out into the roadway and sustained injury to one of his arms. SOUTH LANCASHIRE LIGHT RAILWAYS. It is evident that some steps will shortly be taken in the direction of proceeding with the con- struction of the Electric Light Railway through this district. An official front the Company has been in Earlestown during s this week, enquir- ing as to the ownership of property abutting on the route the line will take, but no information was vou?.hsafed to the property owners as to when it is likely to commence operations. THEFT OF FENCING-.</p>
        <p>Joseph Travis' Millfield-lane, lilaydock, 'a collier, was charged on Thursday at Newton with stealing 90ft. of fencing belonging to William Cawley, farmer, Haydock. The timber was identified by several witnesses, but defendant positively said tirit he purchased part from a colliery, and some he got out of a shed and fence in his garden. He was 1 building a new shed, but prosecutor pulled out the timber alleged to have been stolen and destroyed the shed. He was fined 40s and costs. ST. PETER'S CHURCH BIBLE CLA.B3IS.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening a social gathering of the male and female Bible classes connected with St Peter's Church, Newton, was held in the Town Hall, Newton. The room was very nicely decorated for the occasion, and about 100 of both sexes were present. Dancing commenced at half past six, and was kept up with spirit until 11-30. Mr J Lewis' string band supplying the music At intervals songs were given by Mr Perry and Miss Baybut, and recitations by Mr Mayhew. Tbe refreshment department was under the direction of the Ladies' committee.</p>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARS -HOPE OF EARLESTOWN LODGE.</p>
        <p>The weekly session of the Hope of Earlestown Lodge was held in the Co-operative Hall on Monday evening, Bro S E Greening. CS, presiding over a fair attendance of members. A very interesting letter was read by Bro G Clarke from Bro Joseph Webster of New Z Aland (late of Earlestowni. The report of the Convention, which showed that very steady work is being done in the area, was also read. Ode 138 was sung and readings were given by tiros E Newman, W Rose, J Warburton and G Clarke. A song was also rendered by Bro Rose, which brought a very good meeting to a close.</p>
        <p>BOOS-BINDERS SOCIAL AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>-- On Thursday night the annual "social" promoted by the book-binders employed at Messrs McCorquo- dale and Company's works, was held in the Town Hall, Newton. the room had been very tastefully decorated by Mr Davenport, of Newton, and about 80 people were present. Dancing commenced at 1.30 and was continued with much zest until three clock next morning, the music being supplied by Mr J Lewis' band. The M.C.'s were Messrs E Wolstenholme and G Houghton. Refreshments were supplied by Mrs Greenslade.</p>
        <p>CRUELTY TO HORSES</p>
        <p>At Newton P-e-t-t-y—iiisii-on-9.-o—ti-Yliursday before Messrs T J Gillespie (chairman), 3 Unsworth and W Collinewood, William Broadhurst of Warring- ton, was lined 20s and ceste for working a horse in an unfit state. Constable Wooff and Inspector Print gave evidence, and said that the pony was suffering from bone spavin. It had since been des- troyed.--Llugh Craven and Michael Whelan, of Wigan were charged with a similar offence. In- spectors Limas and Print and Constable Hodgson,</p>
        <p>- of Litte Lever,proved the case, aad said they were en duty at Haydock Park Races on the 11th ult Ddendants drove up with a lead of passengers The horae was very lame and unfit for work. Whelan was ined 20s end Craven 10s. •</p>
        <p>By the Jury : I could not say whether my daughter was discharged cured from Guy's Hospital. Eliza Marsh, mother of the deceased, said she was 19 years old in February last. When she had been there a week she said Mr Chapman had given her a gold watch and chain. I said to my husband, "It seems funny that be has given her that so soon. You had batter go up and see her," and he did so on the Sunday following. When he came back, he said it was quite troe that be had given her this watch and chain, and that he had invited Mr Chapman down on the following Sunday. They both came down on bicycles on the Sunday. Chapman had got two bicycles. I Was anything said about these presents ?—Yes ; I asked my daughter about it. She said it was all right. and that Mr Chapman had given her the watch and chain and some rings which she was wearing. The Coroner here read a letter which witness said she received from her daughter, written from the Mann- intent, Borough. The girl stated that Chapman bad made overtures to her, and sought her mother's advice. Witness replied the same night, telling her daughter to come home at once if she was not comfortable. The Coroner : Then there is another letter here as follows :—" September 12th, 1901. Dear Mother,— Your letter to hand this morning. lam very pleased to say there is nothing between us so far but only friend• ship, and I was silly enough te write that letter to you yesterday. and hardly knew what I was doing. Dear mother, there is no need to worry about me, as I am all right here, and was sorry I wrote the letter to you,as I see different of him this morning. I bad to show th.! letter from you to Mr Chapman, and was surprised at my own folly. After receiving such a nice letter from you last night, Mr Chapman says he will canal down on Sunday if be does not have a different understanding between UP. lam quite comfortable, and lam getting on 611 right. We must take things from a different light from this, but will tell you more when I come to see you. Hoping to bear from you before Sunday, 1 must close with love and best respects from Mr Chapman and myself. —I remain, your loving daughter, Maud • Hoping all is well and papa is better."</p>
        <p>Do you remember receiving that letter P—Yes. I then wrote and said I was glad she was all right, and should be pleased to see them down on the following Sunday. They came down on the following Sunday, and Chap- man expressed a desire to marry my daughter, and went to the hospital to see my husband about it. On returning from the hospital, Chapman took a will from his pocket, made in Maud' favour, which be signed, and I and my son signed it as witnesees. On October 13 I visited them at the Monument. There was a quantity of confetti outside, and my daughter told me that she had been married that morning. My daughter stopped at the Monument until they had a fire there, and then they went to the Crown pablie house. My daughter wrote to say she was ill. I went up and saw Mr Chapman in the bar. I shook hands, and said, " How is Maud f' " He said, "No better." Ha invited ma to go up, and I and my daughter Louisa went up to the back room on ths second floor. There was a nurse named Jessie with my daughter, who appeared very bad. She said she was in great pain,and kept vomiting. Mr Chapman came up while we were there, and she asked for something to drink. He felt her pulse, and did this several times, whenever he came into the room. The nurse said Mr Chapman had given her an inj,ction twice. I stopped there all night. Did you ask her what had made her so ill P—Yes, and she said she thought it was rabbit. I asked her if Mr Chapman had some of the rabbit, and she said they all had some, but that none of the others were ill. When she died the doctor said my daughter had died of poison Just before my daughter died she threw all the clothes off her, and said, " Take them away. I am going. Good-bye al." When Dr Grapple had seen my daughter he called me into a room and said, "Your daughter has been poisoned," or, " Your daughter is being poisoned." I asked him if it was the raobit. He said, "No arsenic." After my daughter's death Mr Chapman asked Dr Stoker for a certificate. Dr Stoker said he did not feel justified in giving one then. Chapman asked ashy. Dr Stoker said there was nothing Co show what she had died from. Mr Chapman said slio had died from exhaustion. Dr Stoker said, " But what caused the exhaustion ?'' Chapman said the continual vomiting and diarrhcea. I suggested that there should be a post-mortem examina- tion. Dr Stoker said if we would agree to a post-mortem examination he would pay all the exoenses himself. I asked 51r Cnapman if he would agree to a post-mortem !examination, and he said, " Yes." Mr Chapman asked inc to stop till the funeral. Inspector Godley told me my daughter was not married. I said to Mr Chapman, " George, haven't you really married Maud? and he said, " No." The Coroner : There are two letters in pencil here, which are both in the same envelope. The first letter is as follows :—" 17, Qaeen Victoria Ward. My dear husband,—Just a line to let you know I am no better. ! I had no sleep last night ; was in pain all night long, I and have not been much te.ter to-day, I came on queer iii the night, and, of coarse, that made matters worse. I will try, dear George, to get better so as to come home and belp you. My own darling husband. I think this is all, so, with love, I remain your ever-tru=. and loving wife, Maud." The second letter is as follows :—" 17. L Queen Victoria Ward. Mr dearest George —A lino as • promised. I had a good night's sleep last night, •°,l feel very much better this morning. The doctor said when he saw rue I had not got a very good prospect, which meant I was not going on so well as he would like Me to do, I think, all this time. So, with fond love, • I remain, yours for ever, Maul, M 9 temperature is r syill going Mgber." • The will referred to by , identified by her. the witness was produced and</p>
        <p>The inquCst or-i;s then adjourned until nevi Tuesday.</p>
        <p>CHAPMAN IN COURT.</p>
        <p>There wag a very large erlwd in S nithwark Paliee Court, on Wednesday,when George Chapman was again</p>
        <p>brought up charged with the murder of Maud Marsh by administering arsenic. - Mr Bodkin now appeared for the Public Prosecutor, and said it was not nis intention to open the case that day, nor to go into any evidence of a' controversial natuto "—if he might use the term. He thought it would be in favour of the defendant himself if the inquest was completed, or adjourned, before the police- court proceedings were gone into at any length. Mr Marsh then went into the witness-box and in the main repeated the evidence he gave at the inquest. F Mr Bodkin referred to the supposed marriage between the dead girl and Ctiapman,and extracted from witness the fact that although he nad been told by his wife that his daughter had been married to Chapman, he never mentioned this to Caapman himself.</p>
        <p>In cross-examination the witness &amp;own-tad that he had never seen any friction between his daughter and Chapman. She appeared to be provided with whatever she asked for.</p>
        <p>When- thiis—witness had st?pped down, Mr Bodkin sug- gested that it would be more convenient to make a tormal remand till next Wainesday, and then to have another remaad of a week, thus fixing the effective hearing for November 26. Mr bydney applied, on behalf of the new tenant of the" Crown," tor permission to remove and store Cnap- . Mall' el property, whica aild been sealed up by the police. This was not opposed by Mr 1; ,dkin, on condition that the police should have reasonable access, and permis- sion was given azeordingly. Tioe prisoner was then remanded tor a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>POISONING MYSTERY.</head>
        <p>THE POISONING MYSTERY.</p>
        <p>The Inquest. --- Evidence of the Parents. ---- Deceased's Letters.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday, Dr. Waldo. the City and Southwark coroner, sat at Bermondsey to inquire into the circum- stances of the death of Maud Eliza Marsh, who died at , the Crown public house, Borough High-street, on October 22. It wilt be remembered that in connection with the ! death the landlord of the Crown, George Chapman, has b !en arrested, and stands remanded in custody.' ! Robert Marsh, father of the deceased woman, in reply , to the coroner, said he visited the &amp;ceased at Chap- ! man's house,and in conversation she said,"slr Chapman has asked me how I would like to be called Mrs Chap- man." I told her she had batter wait and see how she got‘on. I did not see her for sometime afterwirds,when she canoe borne with Mr Chapman. She said she was going to get married, and I told her not to do anything underhanded, but to let me know. I was in the Croy- den Hospital afterwards, and my daughtcr and Mr Chapman dame to see me. No conversation of import- ance took place then, but at a subsequent visit she re- ferred to the marriage,and told me that she was married She had a wedding ring on, and said she had been married on • Sunday somewhere in Bishopsgate, at a Roman Catholic room. I asked her why she did not let me know, and she said Mr Chapman did not want to make a fuss about it.</p>
        <p>Did you ask for the certificate ?—Yes, and she said, " George has got it locked up." Some weeks ago she wrote to her mother saying she was very ill, and was in Guy's Hospital. I went and saw her. I asked her what was the matter, and she said it was inflammation in the inside. She complained of pains in the stomach and retching. I next saw her in bed at the Crown public house on the 18 ch Octob plained of the pains. I asked h she said she could not tell me.</p>
        <p>Did you see your daughter-alone on that weasion ? Yes, but Mr Chapman came in several time. Each tune he came in he walked up to her and felt her pulse. He did not have much conversation with me. I said she seemed bad, and Chapman said, " Yes, she was." She asked for water, and Chapman left the room arid brought it to her. She used to drink a little, and vomit ,it immediately. It was discoloured. , What colour was it ?—lt was • dark colour ; not any particular coloar, but certainly not clear. When did you see her again P—l last saw her on October 21st, at the Crown, and still in bed. When I saw her on that occasion, I thought she was getting better. A nurse named Jessie Trueman was there. I asked my daughter if she wanted anything,and she said, " No." She continued to vomit, and to com- plain ot pains in her stomach. Later on I went back to the bedroom, accompanied by Chapman. I said to him, I believe my daughter will get better now." Chapman sal&amp; " She will never get up any more." I turned to Mr Chapman and asked him if he had seen anyone else like it. He said, Yes." I said, wai your other wife like it ? "</p>
        <p>You still thought your daughtsr was his wife ?—Yes' Was anyboiy else in the room at this time ?—Yes, the nurse Jessie. I had suspicions that my daughter was having foul play. Chapman said she was being attended by a doctor, but I was not satisfied,because his methods of coming in the room, feeling her pulse, and going out to get the water, roused my suspicions. I consulted Dr Grapple,of Croydon, telling him that I had a daughter very ill in Loudon, and I should be pleased if he would I visit her. He went on toe afternoon of October 2l,pay- ing a surprise visit, and I saw him the next morning. He said my daughter was very ill, but he did not see 1 why she should not get well if the sickness weuld stop. He said his opinion was that my daughter had been I poisoned by arsenic, but bow she got it he could not tell. What else passed between you and the doctor ? Nothing else, sir ; but on the afternoon of the same day I received a telegram saying that my daughter had passed away at half-past twelve in the afternoon. I showed Dr Grapple the telegram, and he was very much surprised.</p>
        <p>dross-examined by Mr Sydney: My daughter never complained of the manner in which Mr Chapman treated her at any time. She seemed to have all that she required.</p>
      </div>
