      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>MINOR OASES. --- Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES. --- Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>MoNDAY.—Before Mr F Monks in the chair), rouncillor Smethurst, Messrs Birchall, Hayward, Thornton, J R Young and S Wright. Mtotextr.ntat. LExtvxcv.—Mary Holmes, a stranger to the town, was charged with having been drunk in Bank-street at 11-45 p.m. on Saturday. and with wilfully neglecting mid exposing her three-year-old child.—Prisoner pleaded guilty, —P.S. Smith said he toned the prisoner lying helplessly drunk In Bank-street. It was raining very _heavily and the child was sitting by her side on the wet ground. It, clothes were "dripping 'wet.," Prisoner was so drunk that witness with thimessistanee of P.M. Fish. had to convey her to the Pollee Station upon a hand cart. The child was blue with Cold and was taken to the Workhonse.—Superintendent Monaghan said prisoner came from St Helens on Saturday, but she said she belonged to Liv vpnet.—The Chief Constable remarked that prisoner gave a very unsatisfactory account of herself. She said she was the wife of a Liverpool dock labonrer, but her embserinent, st.tencents led him to doubt this —Da prisoner promising to leave the town she was discharged. I3tPIiTDENT THEFT FROM A SHOP —Margaret Lewis. 260, Rninsford-road. Warringtnn, was charged with stealing a Pair of boots of the valne of 3s 94 from the shop of Alfred Taylor, boot dealer, Horsemarket-atreet. She pleaded grillty.—The Chief Constable said the prisener went into Mr I Taylcr's shop on Saturday, asked the price of a pair of women's boots—very likely far a blind—and while Mrs Taylor went to get the particular class of boot that she wantel the otisoner took the opportunity of stealing a pair , of bey's boots, which she put uuder her jacket. When she Came back, Mrs Taylor heard leather creaking tinder prisoner'" I jacket and said " What have you got under your jacket ? " Prisoner replied" Nothing. "Mrs Taylor said she knewbetter. and on prisoner's jacket being unbuttorned the boots fell to the ground. Prisoner then had the effrontery to'say " Why. I have just bought those boots from yon." Mrs Taylor ordered her to leave the shop and she did so. A policeman was in- formed, and he saw the prisoner join two men in a side street where she appeared to his recounting her adventures in the 'hop. On seeing the officer (P.C. Clarke) the prisoner and one of the men ran away, The officer followed, and after a violent struggle succeeded in bringing the ptisener to the Pollee Station. At first she denied theltheft burulti- mately admitted it. He (Mr Talbot) was of the opinion that the woman was acting in concert with the men. Her hnsband was a soldier. —Prisoner was fined 108 and wits with the option of 14 days imprisonment. FlGHTlNG.—Thigabeth Kearns, IS, Silver-street, and Maria Allsock, 24. Silver-street, were summoned for fighting in Silver-street, on October 28th. P S Evans raid he /*mid the I defendants on the gronnd fighting and pulling each other's hair. With the assistance of several women he separated them, but they again commenced to fight. He then saw them home. but Kearns came out again and smashed seven panes of glass in the other woman's house. Kearns, who made her 20th appearance, was bound over and fined in the costs (7s), while Alleock was also bound over and had to pay 2s costs Joseph Langley, Tinsley's-terrace, and Peter Turner, No 5, , bix Houses, Dallam-lane, were bound over and each ordered I to pay costs, 7s, for fighting. 038TRUCTION ic BRIDGE-BTREET.--Johnson Wilson, cab proprietor, 653. Kuutsterd-read. was summoned for causing an obstruction in Bridge-street on October 21st—P.O. Phillips said that at 2.16 p m. on the date named he Saw the defendant's cab standing opposite the Featner's Motel. , There was no one in charge. As there were no signs of any driver eoming7witness at 250 made Inquiries at the Feathers' Motel and ascertained that defendant was there. When defondant came out witness asked him why be had allowed the cab to stand there so long and he replied " Its all right. Will you have a drink ?" Witness told him that he would not and said he should have to report him. Defendant went, on to the Royal Oak.where he stayed a further ten minutes. —p c Hayes eorreborated.— Defendant, who made his 9th appearance, was fined les and oosts (18s in all).</p>
        <p>FElDAY.—Betcwe the Deputy-Savor (in the chair), Councillor Smetlurst and J Roberts, Messrs Thornton, Hepherd and Wright. THE ATTEMPTED SITICTDE OF • SOLDIER.—Frank Mllls was charged on remand with attempting to committ sulaide . .</p>
        <p>by drowning himself in the Mersey early last Friday morn- ing. The Chief Constable said that Sergeant Barnett saw the prisoner In the river shortly after midnight and made several attempts—the last of which was successful—to get him out. Prisoner was remanded for a week and inquiries had been made as to his history. It appeared that he had been in the arms' for the past ten years. Three years ago he returned home from India and should have been placed on the reserve. On the war in South Africa breaking out, how- ever, he was sent away on active service. He was for a long time In the fighting line. When he came home his pockets were lined with gold which he thought was going to last for at least one man's lifetime. He began the usual course of esjoying himself and painting things in a Colour he ought net to have done.—At this point the Chair- man asked the Chief Constable if he had any suggestion to snake with regard to the prisoner.—The Chief Constable suggested that prisoner should be discharged and handed ever to his father In Liverpool 7-Prisoner said he was very . _</p>
        <p>sorry for what ha had done, and hoped he would never do It again—Prisoner was discharged. Mr Taylor undertaking to take him home to his father and And work for him. BEToND CONTRlL."—Stactey Cooper, aged ten, was Charge] with being found wandering and not under proper ,guardianship,—Mr W Jones. school att,ndance inspector, bslrl the hey was eontinually on the streets end was quits f eyond the control of his parents —The ease was adjourned or a month to give the boy an opportunity of amendment. ALLFOTD WIFE DESERTION.—Richard Kelly, Burtonwood was summoned for deserting his wife Georgina Kelly, Laira-street.—Mr Steel appeared for the complainant and Mr Browne represented the defendant:—Mr Steel stated that defendant left his wife about three weeks age and went to live with his sister at Burtonwood. During that time he had never sent her any money or communiested with her in any way: —For the defence it was stated that prisoner had taken a house at Ihrtonwood but the wife refused to go and live with him.—The magistrates granted a separation order with 10s per week alimony. - --</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>WARRINGI ON SEED GROWERS' ACTION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGI ON SEED GROWERS' ACTION.</p>
        <p>Messrs Gartons' and a Wolverhampton Farmer.</p>
        <p>Before his Honour Judge Bowen Rowlande,K.C., at the Warrington County Court on Thursday, last weekMeesrs Gartons',Limited,seed growere,Warring ton, claimed from Samuel Evans, 33, Dudley-street, Wolverhampton, a retired farmer, the sum of £27 13s 91 for seeds supplied in 1899 and 1900. Mr A Browne appeared for the plaintiffs. and the defend- ant was represented by Mr Liaslam, of Wolver- hampton. Prior to the hearing of the action proper, Mr Haslam ehjected to his Honour's jurisdiction in the case. He contended that the case ought to have been entered into the Wolverhampten County Court. The goods wers or.fered of an agent oi Messrs Gartens', Ltd.. at Wol- verbs mpton and were sent carriage:paid by the plaintiffs to Wolverhampton. Mr Browne said the gentleman who said these goods to the defendant had no power to receive money for them. The money had to be paid to Messrs Garton', Ltd., direct. His Eloriour asked Mr Heslarn what was the objeat of his objection. Mr Haslam said it was a great hardship to bring the plaintiff and his witnesses ta Warrington. Mr Browne said it had been the custom at that Court for the past 30 years to take action in this way. His Ilmour said his inclination was that assuming the affidavit to be correct there was jurisdietion in the War- rington County Court. If the order given by the defend- ant was for a specific thing which was made at a specific place, and to the knowledge of the buyer was en made laird sent from that place, it was part of the contract that they should be sent from that place. Mr Browne said the same point was raised before bis Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes on three separate occas- ions and his Honour always held that when the affidavit disclosed that the goods would be manufactured at Wiarrington that was a material part of the cause of action. No appeal was ever made against his descrsion. His Honour said be held that the Warrington Court had jursidiction in the matter. The claim was then proceeded with. Mr. Browne stated that the claim was for fnur con- signments of seed which were sent on October 7th, 1899, and February 14th, March 13th, and April 4th of toe following year, to the defendant at Wolverhamptdn. The goods were dispatched on the order of Mr Tames, who was the firm's agent at Wolverhampton. Invoices for the goods were forwarded the same night as the goads, in accordance with an invariable custom. Since the goads hid been sent two statements of accounts had been forwarded to the defendant. No objection what- over was made to those statements until May, 1902, when the defendant was asked for payment by ihe firm's new representative, Mr Henry Carter. Defendant said he would Fiend the money to Warrington. Mr Carter pressed for payment, and the defendant promised to pay the money next week. He then said he did not owe the amount in question, and that Mr James had taken back three bags of oats, and that he would not pay for those. Mr Carter told the defendant that Mr James had no authority to take back any oats, and asked him what was the amount he did owe. The defendant replied " I owe about £19." Mr Carter then reported the matter to the firm in writing,and they replied that they had not taken anything hack, but that they were prepared to take £26 aslpayment. The firm then received a letter in Mr James's writing, but signed by Mr Samuel Evans, stating that in compliance with his solicitor's instruc- tions, he enclosed money order of the value of £l7 7s 91, which he said was the amount of his indebtedness to the firm and asked for an acknowledgment. Plaintiffs had never taken back any seeds whatever. Defendant had paid into Court £l7 7s 91 with propor- tion of costs. Henry Carter,teaveller in the employ of the plaintiffs then gave evideece bearing oat this statement. In cross-examination he said defendant did net t him anything abut certain seeds being useless. rg did not vmecnber telling defendant that if he paid £l7 73 91 the account would be settled. He might have told him to send £l9 and see if the firm would accept it. He did not remember defendant stating that there was to be an allowance of six guineas for oats returned and £4 Be for seeds which were practically useless. Harry Pearion, secretary to Messrs Gartons', said he never recsived any complaint from the defendant with regard to any goods being taken back, prior to the report ef Mr Carter. James was not authorised to take back golds.or to give any allowances. Thomas Gsrton,director of Messrs Gartons',said James had no authority to take back goods without the sanc- tion of the firm. For the defence Mr Hatilam said the point was as to whether James had authority to settle the matter as he did in regard to the wheat supplied in October 1899 and he oats supplied in February 1900. The wheat toured out so very badly that James on the authority of Mr Garton arranged with the defendant to do his best to sell it and the defendant got Ll 24 for it. The ca`s were also very had, and James, by an arrangement with Mr Garton, allowed the —claw £6 5i for them. Defendant gave evidence In support of this :state• merit.</p>
        <p>Henry Thomas James crrroborited as to the arrange* meat with the defendant with regard to the bad seeds, which he said was made with the authority of the firm. His Honour held that James had no authority to act as he had done and gave judgment for the plaintiff with ones. He said he was extremely sorry for the defendant and was of the opinion that if James had not interfered, tLe matter would never have come into court. Mr Hastarn applied for leave to appeal on the question of jurisdiction but his Honour refused the applica- tion.</p>
        <p>Nurse Raymond. 33, Dalto-n-itreet, Hulas% Manches- ter, writes:—" I have tested Dr Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, and may say that I have never tasted anything to equal it. I shall bays much pleasure in recommending it to any patients that I nurse." Dr Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, Ltd., has published hundreds of testimonials from nurses, but truly nothing could be stronger than this.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Warrington P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Warrington P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance at an ope n meeting of the above society which was held in the Co- operative Hall, Sankey-street on Sunday. Mr H Thornton occupied the chair, basing his remarks CM temperance. Miss Houghton delighted the audience by her exquisite singing of "The King," and" Daddy," the accompanist being Mr Wads- worth. and also by her creditable rendering of the pianoforte solo, "Storm at Sea," for which she was loudly applauded. The Rev J F Matthews, of Latchford. was the speaker, his subject being " The making of the people Christians." A number of members were enrolled at the close of the meeting. Fairfield Cricket Club. The annual meeting of the Fairfield Cricket Club</p>
        <p>was held at the" King and Queen" Hotel last Fri- day evening. There was a goad attendance of members, and the president, Mr A E Boardman, took the chair, The secretary statel in his report that the weather had been unfavourable for cricket and that three matches had been cancelled in con- sequence of this. However the club had played 14 matches, and of these they had won nine and lost five. The treasurer ?presented his statement of accounts for the season which showed a balance on the right side. A. special vote of thanks was after- wards accorded to :the President, Vice-presidents and officers of the dub for their valuable services during the season. When the business relating to the past season had been transacted the election of officers for the ensuing season was proceeded with.</p>
