      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY NOVEMBER 14 1902</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY NOVEMBER 14 1902</p>
        <p>far as he could make out it was It ,ctor Owen who placed the cupola on the tower, probably in 1770, whea he I rebuilt the nave. It remained for about fifty years, I when it appeared to have been removed, for it was not shown on the print of the church in 1829. Of that second tower, a very heavy building, the greater part was takee down by Rector Quekett in 1860; a portion sad to say,fallinetand bringing with it the western most windows on either aide of the chancel. The present tower, built by Mr Qnekett in 1860, was a beautiful object,and so was the spire by whicu it was surmounted The tower was not originally Wended to carry such a weight, and it was s question he believed still, if it was wise to sing the bolls which were re-hung a little later. It was, mor over, sad that the stone should be so soft tnat it soon crumbled and perished when exprieed to the weather and the corrosive atmosphere of smoke and chemicals. Alter dealing with the Boteler,or Lady °bane, au interesting part of the church, the Rector referred in; conclusioe, to the nave. The nave was all built, with the exception of the so ith aisle, in 1860 and onwards. That had been built in 1835, and was subetantial and good, and money failed. £5,000 had been asked for,ancl £15,000 had been spent, but there were many unfore- seen difficulties, and they could onlyladmire the courage and perseverance ef Quekett. The building, whion was at last completed, was a noble and inspiring edific Be did not know what the style or architeoture was,but tlee windows were intended to correspond with the tracery of those in the Chancel which was decurated,aud were of handsome and graceful design. The windows in ' the south aisle might not have much architectural merit, but they were of great interest an having been those of the nave of 1770, the correeponding ones on the north side having been swept away in 1860. By the generosity , of the congregation mod meaty kind friends near and far £2,000 had just been spent in necessary restoration and most satisfactory improvements. The spire and tower had been re-pointed, and decayed stones replaced. The plaster had beers stripped from the walls of the nave and aisles, and the organ had been removed from the west end to the choir, while additional accommodation had been provided for two hundred an fifty more adults. The Boteler or Lady Chapel has been restored to it's original, and better than its original use. The windows of the south aisle had been reglazed. Only the chancel • remained to be done and that, the most interesting por- tion of the whole church, they hoped in no long time would be worthily restored to the praise and glory of God, and for the benefit and blessings of many genera- tions yet to come. Two beautifully embroidered chasubles which it is believed were found in the Parish Church in 1874, and were handed over by the then Rector, the Rev R A Rawstone to the Rev Father Molyneux, of St Albaa's, and are now in the possession of the Rev Father Whittle, were on view together with the Church Communion plate. On the propesition of Mr T May, seconded by Conn- *Moe BENNETT, and supported by Mr DEWHIIRST, 3 vote of thanks was ac :ordeal to Canon Willis for his lecture, to Mr Houghton for manipulating the lantern, and to Father Whittle for loan of investments.</p>
        <p>In reply to Counoillor Beneett, Cgnoa Virmas said tho spire of the l'a,iFt Church was 297 feet high, and he was told it was the th rd highest in the entatry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The Rector's Interesting Lecture.</p>
        <p>Warrington Parish Church and " its ancient history" formed tie subject of an interesting la:scare delivered to the members of the Warrington Literary and Philoso- phical 836 ty in the Niaaolic Hall. Bold-atreet, on Mon- day evening by the Reoor dtes, Canon Willis). There was a large attendance, snd the leotare,which was illus- trated wich lanern views was listened to with the greatest interest.. . _</p>
        <p>1 The RECTOtt sail the first chi:area Was cloud:rine built by S. Elphis, who flourished as far as they mould tell ,at this spot about the year 700. Theo found in Domes- day Book, aacordiog to the late Mr Beamout, that St. Elphin hold a carucate of land free from all tax except tee geld, which wag the ampunt uznally given for the erection of a church. What the building was like and of what mat,riale cornoosel they could only eonjecture. Antiquariani suggested that it was probably constructed of mud and wattle. P-iesing over a perio lof three huodred years, Canon Willie gave a list of the great families who successively became patrons of the Pariah Church, owners of Bawsey and lords of the Manor of Warrington. The first was the Da Viler.. Soon after the Conquest, in the time of William Rufus, Roger of Poieton. Earl of Lancaster, gave the Master of Waring- ton to Paganus de Viler% who built his castle en the Mote Hill, cno, sill of Amenably, where the Clergy Daughters Scheel and the College now stand. Paganus handed on his property to his son Pdattbceus, and from then toe patronage and rnanoral rights passed by marriage into the family of the Betelers, who held them for four hundred years, from 1150 to 1550, when they passed by purehage to the Dudley, in the person of , Robert Dudley, Earl of Leicester, son of John Dudley, Duke of Northumberland, the notorious Earl is Sir Waiter Scott's celebrated novel Kenilworth, who murdered his first wife Door Amy Rebeart. Then they went to his brother Ambrose, Earl of War wiok. and then to his son Robert, by his second wife. Lady Sheffield. Robert Dudley sold the patronage and manorial rights to the Irelands, and for two generations they remaioed with them, and then earns a division of those proprietary rights the peronagri and ovrostship of Bowsey, to which place Sir William Fitz A.lmerin Ds Boteler, the seventh baron, had transferred his seat from the Mote Flill,going by marriage of an heirese to the Athertons, and the manorial rights tor purchase to the Booths. Lord 11,1a- mere from whom they were bought by the Blackburnes, and from them, in 1851, by the Corporation of Warriag- ton, who in their armorial hearings, have preserved the liens of the de Vilars and the cups of the Botelere. The patronage with the residence and preperty at /horsey, meanwhile, passed again by the marriage of an heiress from the Athertone to the Gwillyms, and from them once more by marriage to the Powys Lard Lilford, in whose cossearon the patronage and property has sines remained. ,</p>
        <p>Coining to the churchea, Canon Willis sale they knew four had good upon the present site. The first was a INorman building erected about 1150 by Matthceus de Tilers who granted the alivowson in 1167 to the prier of Thargatten or Fiskerton-on-Trent, in Nottingham- shire. That church stood for About two hundred years until 1350, the patronage being reconveved about 1250 ley Adam, prior of Thurgetton, to Sir William Fits Almeria ie Boteler, the seventh baron—rhe on• sho removed his seat frene the Mote to Bewrey. OF that church some stones were Will to be seen in the ' Warrington Museum. It was taken down abut 1350, and in its place was built the second church In the Decorated style of architecture by Sir William Fitz William Is Beteler, the ninth baron, in 1354, of which the ohancel still remained. The nave, after standing over four hundred years was taken down in 1770. From a drawing of the chunk :it could be seen it was cruel- form without side aisles, the Hotelier and Massey chapels forming the arms. The architects of the present shurela, hewever, said they found when excavating for the bases of the pillars in 1860 distinct traces of the nave and side :Ogles of the original oleurch of 1354. In 1770 the third nave was built by the Rev Edward Owen which lasted till 1860, but in 1855 Rector Pewys took down the south wall of the nave and built with the same stones the present south aisle, allowing the date of the first erection to remain, which was very misleading and had already caused a little very friendly contro- versy. A good idea of the building could be formed from a study of the south aisle, for the north wall of the nave corresponded exactly to the south, as might be seen from the photographs still in existence taken by Mr Quekett before he began his restoration. In 1860 and onwards the f north church was built, that was to say all that was now visible except the chancel and the south aisle. It was a noble building, and though the nave, spire, and chancel were all out of propertien and the absence of otereetory windows darkened the interior and the galleries were net ornamental, the general effect, bath externally and internally, was very Impressive, and really wonderful considering the time at which it was dene and the unacelesiastical feeling of the age. _ _</p>
        <p>The Rector dealt with the chaneel at seine length,peint- ing out that it was begun in 1354 by 9ir William Fitz William Is Roteler, the ninth haren. Viewed externally it would seem that the four topmost cowries had been added at a late date, and there was a large I-attracts en the north side and four windows at the eastern end and sides almost en a level with the ground. There were else deep impressions in the etone-work at the east end. All these told their own tale. Ti. first that the chaneel was probably mush lower and the rest of the building small in propesition when originally eon- struated in 1354. The laet were the evidences Cromwell'e handiwork when his soldiers were encamped on the Mount where the Clergy Daughters School and the Training College now stand. Bat the buttress and the windows were the interesting featnree. They were connected er,th a crypt whisk was ban at the same time as the chancel. The only access to that chamber originally was by a staircase within the buttress. For 200 years it must have been regularly used by the parish priest both as an oratory and a vestry, but On account of the flour windows and the diffieult, nee impossible, approach, not as a mor- tuary chapel. That staircase not only descended into the crypt, but ascended towards the roof, but for what pv-pose was indeed a mystery. It might have been as some thought to obtain access to the roof left, but no instance was known of a rood screen so far east or it might have led to an opening in Nag wall above for the exposition of relics, er to a wall pulpit, or simply have given access to the roof for repairs. It was impossible t• say. The crypt was closed from 1580 to 1824. It was probably in 1560, the first year of Queen Elizabeth, in which the order for the removal of rood screens and other ornaments of the chamber was issued that the en- trance was blooked up, and for the space of more than two hundred and fifty years the staircase and crypt were apparently unknown, the earth probably having accumulated round the windows. It was in the year 1821 that the discovery of the existence took place in the incumbency of the Rev Robert Atherton Rawsterne, partly by the removal of some panelling whiela had beers pieced round the chancel, and partly through the piercing of the buttress, but nothing was done for eleven years until tip tints of Mr Horace Powys. who succeeded in 1832 and was a great builder. In 1811. Mr Powys made the staircase in the south side. In order to de that,he closed a small entrance into the chaneel, where the sedilia now were,and made the steps down int* the crypt instead of up into the chancel ; and having blocked up that door be made another much larger a little to the west just outside the communion rails, so for the first time making a public entrance into the crypt from the south. Another feature connected with the oliancel was, he regretted to say, that his pre- deeeseer availed himself ot the stairease previously mentioned, made an entrance into a vault at the foot of the steps and, emptying it of its remains, turned it into a chamber for 3 heating apparatus. Mr Qeekett suc- ceeded in 1854, and soon began to make alterations. Just as Mr Powys raised the roof of the obaneel, se Mr Qnekett raised the floor and placed within it the hand- some oak stalls, and in order to give more room ta the communicants he pushed back the steps and the rails 18 inches, which, most unfortuaately, necessitated the re- moval of the entrance into the buttress staircase a similar distance ; and then in order to obtain sufficient room for ingress and egress he hacked off the eastern side of tic interior of the buttress—a most sad mutilation. He also cut off the steps ascending from the filer of the chancel to the top of the buttress. This to their great esrrow they di,icovered when they rerneved the plots: last year. To so ne extent Mr Quekett atoned for this by regrothing eel tiling tbo crypt, which apparently Mr Powys had left much as he found it, though he used it as a vestry. _ . . . _</p>
        <p>the tower of the church of 1354 was shattered by Crom- well's cannon balls in the Civil War. It was taken down in 1636 by Rector Shaw and rebuilt by him in 1693. At the same time bells were east and bunt in the tower. As</p>
        <p>t, ',)'4;zl:z.,,' • , • ,?.'"7"7-', L- '</p>
