      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>SEVENTY-SEVEN CASES OF NOSE AND THROAT CATARRH CURED,</head>
        <p>SEVENTY-SEVEN CASES OF NOSE AND THROAT CATARRH CURED,</p>
        <p>The failure of the usual medical and operative trnt- nsent for catarrh of the nose and throat is well illus- trated in an analysis of 77 cases of catarrh of the nose and throat cured by the Drouet method. These cases are taken from the records of the Drouet Institute,datiog from the first of this year, and art the subject of com- ment in the " Journal for the Deaf," an analytical quarterly review of diseases of the ear,noae, and throat. In reviewing these cases the editor presents forcibly the contrast between the success of the mild but thorough method advised b 7 the Drouet Institute, and the non- success of operations and cauterisation. Those who de- sire to investigate the Druuet method, can obtain a free copy of the "Journal " by addressing the Secretary, Drouat hatitute, 72, Regent's Park-road, London, N.W. With the " Journal "is always enclosed a Report Form, t-..y means of which anyone can obtain free advice by correspondence, and instructions for using the treat- ment at home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Welsh Church, Crosfield-street</head>
        <p>Welsh Church, Crosfield-street</p>
        <p>The other evening a suceessf ul concert in aid of the church funds was held; and was well attended Miss Maud Jones. R.A.M.,Llangollen (soprano),sang with pleasing effect "The Promise of Life," "Y Gardetes Fach," and "14y Dearest Heart," and was heartily encored. Madame Parry, Mold (contralto), rendered with much taste and sweetness, " The Long Avenue,"" Oh, dry those tears," and "God's Slumberland," and was enthusiastically :recalled, Mise Daisy F Smith, of the Liverpool Prob 3 n Orchestra, is an accomplished violinist the composi- tions "Ballade at Polonaise,"" Gipsy dance," &amp;c., being played in a masterly and effective manner. Miss Maud Lawson (Liverpool), elocutionist, was very successful in " Trouble in the Amen Corner," and" In the usual way," which were well received Messrs Val Thompson (tenor) and George Rowlands (baritone), both of Liverpool, rendered "Eileen Mevourneen," " An Evening snug," " Blew, blow, thou Winter Wind," and "The Mighty Deep," in a manner which called forth unstinted applause. Mr B Wadsworth supplied the accompaniments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>SCIENCE KILLS SUPERSTITION.</head>
        <p>SCIENCE KILLS SUPERSTITION.</p>
        <p>William, Duke of Normandy, first landed in Englin&amp; in revensey, on the 28th of September, 1086. On that day it was a matter of doubt,whether he would earn the title of William the Conqueror or William the Vanquished. To his superstitious followers the latter seemed the more probable event.for at the very moment of his landing the Duke's foot slipped and he fell with both lands upon the ground, whereat his followers, regarding the circumstance as an evil omen, raised a loud cry of grief. But the ready wit of the Duke put a different complexion on his fall. "By the splendour of God." he shouted, "I have taken seizen (possession) of my kingdom ; the earth of England is in my shandi." ,In the dark days of INS, and for centuries afterwards, sickness was regarded rac:e or less as a divine visitation, and it Was thought wicked to do anything Co avert or remove it. We know better new. Superstition has been slain by Science, and there are but few human ailmenti tor which Nature has not pro- vided a remedy, it being the task of Science to discover it. Miss EliZl Peters, of Tideford, St Germans, writing on Suns 16th, 1902, says: "I can honestly testify to lbs effioacy of Mother Seigel'a Curative Syrup. I was but a girl when first troubled with indigestion. I en- joyed good health up to a certain period, when my appetite declined, and the little I ate caused dreadful pain in the elect, some spells lasting as long as two bun—not acute pain, but pain of that gnawing and persistent character so hard to bear, tendering one's life miserable. When I ate more often than not my heaft would palpitate alarmingly. I always seemed to be labouring tinder the unreasonable dread of some impending evil, and though I slept very well,l was slways drowsy and exhausted in the oncrnMg,,and could not bring myself to take proper interest in life. Such WM arty condition for months, growing worse day by day, until I became so thin and weak that I could hardly get about. In short, I suffered from indigestion in its worse form, and nothing which I attempted alleviated my misery in the least, But now Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup has changed all that. A neighbour, to whom I can never be sufficiently grateful, reetrismanded its use to me—a rezommendation based upon a happy experience of its power for good. It took several bottles to absolutely mare me ; but my cure, once effected, was complete and hating, so that all I new know of indigestion is confined exclusively to memory."</p>
        <p>The resignation was received on Wednesday under un- usual circumstances of Dr R Bishop as a member of the Manchester City Council. Dr Bishop was chairman of the Electricity Committee, and on representations being made to the committee by the vice-chairman he found it desirable to refund some of the money received in con- nection with the expenses of a deputation to London. Councillor George iworth has succeeded Dr Bishop in he chairmanship of the Committee.</p>
