      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>ELECTION OF MAYOR.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF MAYOR.</p>
        <p>Meeting of Warrington Town Council.</p>
        <p>Alderman Roberts's Resignation.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pemberton Objects to a Standing</p>
        <p>Order.</p>
        <p>Mr J Charlton Parr, J.P., C.A., DL, was unani- mously re-elected Mayor of the Borough at the annual meeting ef the Warrington Town Council held at noon on Monday. More than usual interest was taken in the proceedings and there was a large attendance of the general public. The Mayoress and Mr Roger Parr were amongst these present. The members of the Council and officials present were :—The Mayor (Mr J Charlton Parr), Alderman Sutton, Fairclough, Francomb, Hutchinson, Boltoa, Heaton,Shaw (rreen, Monks, Councillors Smethurst, Bennett, John Evans, Pierpoint, Rogers, Tinnion, Ellison, Duncan, Burton, Peacock, F R Roberts, 3 Roberts, James Evans, Baird, Wilkinson, Davies, Forshaw. Sampson, W Roberts, Brophy, Brough, Barton, Dodds, Upson, Pemberton and Craik, Mr J Lyon Whittle (town clerk), Mr Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk), Mr J Fairhurst (borough treasurer), Mr T Longdin (surveyor), Dr J G Gomel (medical officer), Mr C Madeley (librarian), W fi Grimsdale (electrical engineer). THE RECENT'• ELECTIONS. _</p>
        <p>The Tows CLERK read the returns of the recent municipal elections. Mr Parr then announced that the next business was the appointment of Mayor and he would leave the Couneil to their delibera- tions. The MAYOR then retired. TELE MAYOR RE-ELECrED.</p>
        <p>Alderman &amp;mote, the father of the Council, was then appointed to take the chair pro tern. Alderman Suoroor,haring taken the chair,eaid he had much pleasure in moving "That Joseph Charlton Parr, Esq. be and he is hereby re-elected Mayor for the ensuing year, and until the accept- ance of office by his successor." At the last regu- lar meeting °Mho Council, Alderman Sutton con- tinued,a resolution was passed thanking the Mayor for his past services, when he again expressed his intention te accept office for another year, in com- pliance with toe unanimous wish of the Council, and with the memorial which had been presented to him. He (Alderman Suton) thought they ought to congratulate themselves that the Mayor had given such consent—(hear, bear)—becaule there could be no question or doubt that the Mayor had rendered very valuable services indeed to the town, not only during his mayoralty, but on many occasions before. (Hear, hear and applause.) Alderman SHAW GREEN seconded the resolution, and said the heartiness with which the Mayor agreed to accept office for another year showed that his heart was in agreement with the work that had to be done by them as a Corporation. (Hear, hear.) The Mayor had referred to the number and the magnitude of the duties the Corporation hao under- taken' and many of whichiwould be completed dur- ing the year now entered upon. It was a pleasure to them to know that Mr Parr was at the head of affairs to see those things through, and they would all feel grateful to him for having again accepted the office at their reqaest. (Hear, hear.) The resolution was unanimously carried with acelammatinn, and the Chairman and Alderman • Shaw Green proceeded to the Mayor's parlour for tho purpose of installing the Mayor. On re-entering the Council Chamber, the Mayor was received with hearty applause. Raving signed the weal declaration of acceptance of office, he said that in once more accepting tho honour and assuming the responsible position just conferred upon him as he was told, by the unanimous vniee of the Council, of the mayoralty of their county borough, he wished first to thank the Council collectively, and individually, very sieenrely for the honour and consideration whish, he took it, that vote meant. Since they last met as a Council some of their old friends had fallen in the municipal battle, friends with whom they might be allowed to express sineore regret that they were no longer with them. But that would not prevent them from giving a very hearty and friendly welceme to throe whom the fortunes of war and the votes of the ratenayers had given them in their places. (Hear, hear). In again accedting the honour of the mayoralty he did an. he might say, with less di ffi leoce and more nowildence than he did twelve months ago, because he felt that the experieece !mined and knowledge arquired would enable him to have a batter gra..p af the dutieq entailed. Though Mere' quite true that a Reload year of office might seem more difficult and exacting, from having bet the glamour and preita- meat and nevelty, still, on the other hand, he felt very erenely the counteryailine advantego Chat whereas this time last rear he was a eomp tretive stranger to his Council and his Council to hire, at the present moment IR. felt he had a personal friend in every member of the old Council, and trusted that bnfore the year was out, he might say the same of these new members who it was now his erivilege te weicnme. (sear, hear.) Their Council is the year new past harthe ventured to believed endeavoured to exercise its functions carefully, dis- creetly, honestly, and economically. They had worked together without friction and discord, and they had, he thought, the interest of the town and its many and varied requirements first and foremost in their minds. They had elan endeavoured to put aside all private and personal feeling, all political bias and party censideratiens, all religious differences and sectarian prejudicee. Their policy had been, while holding their own opinions, to respect those of others, arid to give credit to each and all for wishine and trying to do their very best to servo the interest"; of the tows and the welfare of the ratepayers generally. He felt sure that the same good intentione which had actuated their Council in the past would animate their new Council this year, so that at the close they might have a reward of time well spent and work carefully and conscientiously done. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>APPOINTMENT OF DEPUTY-MAYOR.</head>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF DEPUTY-MAYOR.</p>
        <p>To MAYOR appoiated as his deuut• for the ensuing year, Aldsrnsan James Fairelnueh. (Applanes.) Alderman FAIRCLOUSR said he appreciated that honeur. aad Ise assured the Mayor that he would de all bn could tn make his,duties as pleasant as possible. (applause.) THE MAYOR'S CHAPLAIN.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR ti,-;nap-oin-tit-d-tli-dit-e;Ya-n-On" Willis ALA., al his chaplain for the+ miming velar. MR. PEMBERTON AND TOE PRINTING OF MINUTES.</p>
        <p>Mr PEMBERTON Mid he would like to say a word OR the standing orders as he wished to prows an alteration to standing order number 9. It read at present as follows :—"The Town Clerk shall issue with the Coun- cil sunarnoni a print of all important minutes passed by the committees to be taken into eonsideratien by the Council. The Chairman of eash committee to decide which minutes are so to be printed." Be thought that ought to be altered to read that all minutes required by law to receive the sanction of the Council should be printed. The MAYOR : lam informed that all required by law are printed. Conneillor PEMBERTON • We don't knew. That is the point. If that is so there can be no objections te its being put in the standing order. The MAYOR : Any member hss access to the minutes in case he wishes to commit them.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : said the point was that the standing order gave the Chairmen of the committees power to decide which minutes were to be printed. He irioved that all the minutes be printed instead of leaving it to the discretion of the Chairmen of eommittees. Councillor Ursox said that in order than the m ttter oeuld be thrashed out he would irecond the proposition. Alderman SUTTON said Councillor Pemberton admit- ted that he knew nothing at all about this 'natter, and he (Alderman Sutton) should respectfully evegest that be made himself better acquainted with the rules and regulations before Ise made any proposition at all. He (Alderman Sutton) thought that would be far better, because he thought Councillor Pemberton's experience would tell bitn hereafter that everything which the law required was printed in the minutes. He di i not think it would be necessary for Councillor Pemberton to make any remark whatever when be got better informed as to</p>
