        <p>the preeeedings of the Council and of every committee. The MAYOR pointed out that the standing orders could be amended ;at any time, and possibly it was hardly requisite to move an alteration at this very early stage. Councillor PEMBERTON said he had objected to the standing7order publicly, and he did not think he would be doing his duty if he did not take the first opportunity of objecting to it in the Council. He could not see his way to withdraw the Mono's.</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON said the proviso was put in the stand in order to pri vent any trivial things of no conse- quence and of no public interest being printed in the minutes. Everything important was bound to Is printed, and no chairman ever offered to veto anything. Councillor BIERPOINT suggested that Councillor Pem- berton should agree to the withdrawal of the resolution. He would have aa oppertunity at the next monthly meeting of formulating a resolution whioh perhaps might suit his purpogo better than pressing the matter home that day. He hoped Councillor Pemberton would see his way to withdraw the metier' till a more fitting:time for discussion than the present. Councillor PEMBERTON agreed to withdraw the motion until the _next meeting. . _</p>
        <p>Councillor lIPsoN alio consented to thts course, and the standing orders were approved.</p>
        <p>rin APPOINTMENT OF COMMITTEES.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said the resignation of Alderman Roberts, about which he would have something te say later ea, bad created a vacancy on several committees, and he suggested that a special meatier, should be called to fill them up. He had received a letter from Councillor Dodds, which stated that be had decided not to accept office as a member of the Rivers Committee, for which committee his name was put forward on the 13:11 inst. In that case, continued the Mayor, there would be another vacancy to be filled up. Councillor TINNION IDI you mean a special meeting of the Council or special meeting of the General Pur- poses CsrnmitW3</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: Of the Council. Councilior PIERPOINT : Ain Ito understand that the Wateh Committee w.ll be incomplete until tbst meeting is held P,</p>
        <p>The Tows CLERK : We meet on Thursday. and we proceed with the app intent of Chairrnaa and Deputy Chairman.</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON rlse.to make a sugzeation,</p>
        <p>Cunillr PIERPOINT (interrupting);;id : You want ta teach the Council everything. This is an incident that bas never occurred before. You are creating a precedent, and we want to koow how it is pigs to work out ?</p>
        <p>The MaToe : It hair been euggeeted that the meeting of the Town Council be calle efere the committee meetings. are held.</p>
        <p>COUIcSiIIOP BENNETT : But one or two committees meet this afternoon on which a vacancy occurs. Councillor PIERPOINT : Now there is another point. I talc.' it that the resignation of Alderman Roberts is com- plete in every legal sense and no resolution of this Council can alter it. The only way Alderman Roberts can re-enter this Cmincil I suppose, is through the medium of a vacancy being created in a ward through the appointment of ao Alderman. He could then go in by the vote of the burgesses and not otherwise. I fail to see why you wish to postpone the appointment of these cotnrnittoes.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : There will be six committees to fill up. We thought it would he rather difficult at a large meet- ing of this kind to find gentlemen to fill all the vacancies. If the Council wish, it is entirely ia their hands to take what course they think proper. Couno llor Trimmer proposed that the vacancies be filled up at that meeting. Councillor WILKINSON emended. Councillor BENNETT rht raises =other point as te the filling no of the Aldermanic seat. The MAYOR: That cannotlcome up for another fonrteen days. It rests with you to say whether the vanaucies hall be filled before the appointment of Alderman. It as to cover the difficulty that we suggested that a weeniet meeting shoulsthe held. Councillor PIERPOINT : I think you would meet the d ffioulh7 if insl,iiAci of eopyintin,e the Oheirtnah aod</p>
        <p>Deputy-Chairman of committees at your firat nseeting ,ou postpone action uatil the committees are filled up. The VietY3Ft : ThA stanches:: orders require us to make the appointments of Chairman and Deputy-Chairman at once.</p>
        <p>Th. DBPITIT-MAYIR (ti ) Councillor Piornoint) : I suggest that a special meeting of the Counoil can be hold et the °lose of thia meeting. Are yeu agreeable to that ?</p>
        <p>u„ ouncillor PIERPOZNT : I agree to that. Councillor TINNION : I fail to see why we casuist fill the committess rip at tha arniunl meeting.</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON All it requires is for someone to propose a member in Alderman Roberts's place on the Watch Committee and follow it through with the other committees.</p>
        <p>Aidarnlan SUTTON' : It kali always bees easterner): to fill up these vacancies . • meetinz prior to the annual meeting of the Connell and we do that in order to do away with as much unolcasaatness as possible. Councillor Pierpsint knows this as well am anybody only he like. ts make a mountain et-0.-aanlehill.,..—Mau ter.) He likes to hays a goel deal to say about netblbVtbink the StMges9— Mon that we have a meeting after this, will meet all re- quirements. Couneillor PIIMPOINT : There is only one preposition before the Connell.</p>
        <p>,e.) Ile likes to r-thinTl the eattges-</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON supported the preposition. Councillor TINNION : Let the Council decide it. Alderman SUTTON : They have decided it over and over again. Councillor TINNION This used to be done at the annual meeting but to avoid some unpleasantness and unnecessary personal discussion it wa. resolved that we should have a special meeting of the Council. But still it has always been understood that eye, that has not been binding on the members of the Council. It should not be necessary for us to have a motion of this kind to proceed in the usual form. Alderman SUTTON : I propose that the vacancies be filled up rt a meeting to be held directly after the con- clusion of this meeting. The DEPUTY-MAYOR seconded. On a vote being taken the amendment was carried.</p>
        <p>The Council the-ti proceeded to appoint the committees leaving the vacancies to be filled at the subsequent meeting. When the names of the members of the Museum Committee came up for csafirination, CoUtleiller PIERPOINT remarked that Alderman Roberts and had always taken a great active interest in the In- stitution, and he should be sorry to see his clunaltiou with the Corporation absolutely severed, and there seemed to be a probability that it would be severed. He, therefore, proposed that he be cleated a member of the , MU,IPUITI Committee as a noa-tnember of the Council.</p>
        <p>COLIOCIBOr BENNETT Beclouded. Alderman BOLTON and Councillor SMETHURST supported and the motion was agreed to. On the subjoe.t of the Markets Committee, Mr Forshaw expressed kis willingness to retire in favour of Councillor 'James Evans, as there seemed to be a desire that that gentleman should be a member of the co m- mittee. Aldermen HUTCHINSON said he had no objectiom, hut It was not usual for a councillor to retire and. propose another councillor. (Laughter.) The changes was agreed to. In reference to the Gymnasium Committee, Councillor 'Ft/glum/ said he was very sorry to learn that Die Daniel Jones's health was in such a condition that be would be prevented from working with the committee.</p>
