      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>SUBSTITUTE FOR BREAD.</head>
        <p>A SUBSTITUTE FOR BREAD.</p>
        <p>"X have been a student of the food question for years, and know what a hollow sham—regarded from a dietetic standpoint—ordinary (white) bread is. A better substitute for it than !FORC_IV cannot conceive of."</p>
        <p>(Name furnished on application.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <p>Franoomb; St Austin's, Alderman Shaw Green ; and Latchford, Alderman Bolton. REPRESENTATIVES OF THE CORPORATION. Alderman SUTTON moved that Alderman Francomb be appointed to represent the Corporation on the Lanea shire Asylums Board for 1902-03. He did not think that he need talk about increasing the salary, for Alderman Francomb paid all the expenses out of big own pocket. (Laughter.) That would be some information to the public. Alderman BOLTON seconded, and observed that Alder- man Few:womb would be the right man in the right plane.</p>
        <p>The resolution was carried. Alderman Fee Newell, in reply, said that for many years he was the representative of the Board through being a member of the County Council. During that time be aould not charge, but at the present time he could charge railway expenses on account of represent- ing the borough. (Hear, bear). Alderman Heaton was appointed the representative on the Lanoashire Inebriates Acts Beard, and Alderman Shaw Green on the County Rate Joint Committee. THE RESIGNATION OF ALDERMAN ROBERTS. HIS PAST SERVICES ACKNOWLEDGED. The MaYon said : I have an announcement to make, whieh lam sure will be received with much regret bY the Council. I have had placed in my hands the re- signation of the Deputy-Mayor, Alderman Roberts, but his reasons for taking this course have been so fully ex- plained by his published letter that I feel it unnecessary to refer to them here. lam sure, however, we may express our grateful thanks for the good work he hae done, and our very great regret that lae has considered I it necessary to sever his conneetion with the business of I the Corporation. Personally, I feel that I have lost a loyal and staunch supporter in my late deputy, and one I who was always ready and willing to help me whecever and wherever I needed his assistance. (Hear, bear.) I move this resolution :—" That thia Council receive with regret the resignation of Henry Roberts, Esq., of the office of alderman, and desire to place on record their warm appreciation of the eminent and valuable public services rendered by him during his connection with the Town Council for upwards of 18 years." The DErury-Maros seconded.</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON in supporting the resolution said II do so becaase it is my experience of something like ,130 years in this Council that I never knew a more active lor a more intelligent member than Mr Roberts has been during the 18 years he has been connected with it. He has rendered very valuable services on most of the 11111- portant committees connected with the Corporation,and I am quite sure that not only the Council but the whole of the town have suffered a great loss by his resigna- tion. (Hear, hear, and aoplause). Councillor BENNETT : I think the resolution is one of such moment that it ought net to be passed with a mere perfunctory expression of regret. I don't prophse to enter into any of the controversial questions which have occupied the publie mind during the past few monther or to express any opinion as to the wisdom or the unwis- dom of the decision Mr Rober s:has come to,but I should like to say as one who has had considerable opportuni- ties of judging of the work he has rendered to the town and Corporation that I don't think within my own ex- perience that any member has worked harder or with greater intelligence and zeal for the true interests of the town than the late Alderman has done. (Hear, hear). I have differed from him on many occasions, sometimes as you know, rather warmly, but a common sense of generosity as between man and man prompts one to say he has rendered the most valuable services possible, and the whole of the town will receive his:resignation on these grounds at any rate, with regret. (Hear hear)</p>
        <p>The resolution was unanimously agreed to.</p>
        <p>The fdavon declared the office to be vacant. The vacancy would be filled up at the meeting of the Coun- cil to be held on the 2nd December next. THE MAYOR AND CORPORATION AT CHURCH. The MAYOR intimated that he intended to attend Divine service at the Parish Church on Sunday morning next at 10.30. He should be glad if the membere of the Council and representatives of the town would seeora- pany him as usual and assemble at the Town Hall at 10 o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>COUMITTEE9.</head>
        <p>THE COUMITTEE9.</p>
        <p>Tho business of the annual meeting having been cOol- pleted, the Council resolved itself into committee for the purpose of filling up the vacancies on the several com- mittees occasioned by the retirement of Alderman Raberts. The following appointments were made : Wenn Corsanrrun.—Alderman Monks. HISALTH COMMITTISE.-00lltleillOr Brough.</p>
        <p>MUSEUM CommirrEE.—Councillor James Dodds.</p>
        <p>RIVERS Commun.—The place ve3ated by Alderman Heaton, Alderman Roberts and Councillor Dodds were filled by the appointment of Councillors James Evans, Forshaw and John Roberts. FIRE ENGINE CIMMITTEE.--.COUTICHIGT BrOtlel. WATER COMMITTEE —COUIIOIIIOT Pemberton. TRAMWAYS Comssirrns.—Alderman Bolton.</p>
        <p>STOP A COUGH IN ONE NIGHT. A DOCTOR'S TESTIMONY. VEND'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE is a scientific remody, etannearlso.6l. f coo tea beillinnt, crf bronchitis, asthma, citarrh, influenza and childreto coughs, far superior to ordinary cough mixturms, or an! of the emulsions. George Knox, M.D., 256, seeps,' AvenuP, Pittsburgh, Pa., writes " Velso's Lightning Coug, Cure excels in chronic coughs and bronchitie,sal is especially safe ter ohildreo." Price Is. lid. and 2s. 91 B,ld everywhere by chemists and medicine vendors.</p>
        <p>School Attendance Officers' National Association. The annual meeting of the Warrington and Dis- trict Branch of tke'alsove Association was held atthe School Attendance Offices, Warrington on SatardaY Mr W Jones (president) in the chair. Owing to the inclement weather there was only a moderate attendance. The Secretary's report was read from which it appeared that the Warrington Branch wee in a favourable coalition having an increase of fear in membership on the previous year and a balance in hand. It was decided that Messrs W Jones, Bamber and T Martindale represent the Warrington and District Branch at the annual meeting of the Manchester and District Branch in December. The following officials were elected for the ensuing , year :—President, Mr W Janes (Warrington); Vice' President, Mr Thomas Martindale (Earlestown) Secretary, Mr W Rutter (Warrington) ; Treasure' Mr W Bamber. (Warrington.) Committee :—Messrg T Bromilow (St Helens), 0 Davies (Widnes), 0,„ Howard (Runcorn), A' Pasquill (Golborne) and H Scarisbrick (Warrington). Wycliffe Young Peop'e's Society. On Monday evening, the lady members of the above-named society were very kindly entertained by those of the opposite sex. The first part of the programme eonsisted of a lecture on " Art," tbe subject being illustrated by some very laughable "Tableaux vivants." Mr Gauld proved himself to be an excellent " professor " and the various parte were well performed. During an interval in the programme refreshments were served. " Flowery " sketch entitled " The mischievotig bonquet "occupied the second half of the entsr- tainment. The artistes were Messrs A E Longshavit W Booth, FBrown, H Hill and C H Bindlose. The performance was well given and delighted the audience. Upon the proposition of Miss E seconded by Miss A Beswick a hearty vote of thank; was given to the gentlemen for their kW' hospitality.</p>
        <p>Local Commissions. The following changes and appointments ate notified in the 'Gazette": Militia.-3rd Battalion, the King's (Liverpool Regiment): Supernumerst9 Captain J W Fish, from the 4th Volunteer Battalion the King's (Liverpool Regiment), to be lieutenant. 2nd Yolunteer Battalion, the Prince of Walesl Volunteers (South Lancashire Regiment) : Lied; tenant W N Pilkington to be cantain : secon? lieutenants to be lieutenants, G K Chambers, a Caldwell, and R Callan&amp;</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FAlLS:MEL—According to " Kemp's Me" cantile Gazette," the number of new Bankruptcv pro- ceedings in England and Wales wizened during week ending November Bth, was :,89. The number the corresponding week of last year was 1902, to dste. increase of 15, being a net increase hi of 26.</p>
