      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>JOHN GARLICK'S WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>JOHN GARLICK'S WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>CREDITORS.</p>
        <p>A Supplemental Dividend.</p>
        <p>Mr C J Dibb (thene official receiver in bankruptcy for this district) issued a statement to the hn Garlick Creditors of Jo otherwise John Grant, a bankrupt, showing the position f date of declaring a Supplemental estated iv id en dlt tt will he be rernemade a that at the time John Garlick a hurried departure from Warrington to evade arrest, he was heavily involved with a number of Warrington creditors whom he had traded with in connection with his " busi- ness " at Rattersby-lane under the name of John Grant. When the estate was wound Up, with Mr Robert Lo we as trustee, dividends amounting to 2s a i l n the were paid to the unsecured creditors. It was then thought that this was an end of the matter, but subsequently Gull* escaped from Stafford gaol and re-started business on a large scale at Hull under the name of John Brooks. It eventually leaked out that he was the John Garlick who wae "wanted " by the and he was obliged to leave 'Hull as speedily as he had quitted Warrington. Ile did not, however, escape arrest and he is now safely lodged in4l gaol. Being an undischarged bankrupt, Garlick's Dull estate was successfully claimed by Mr Bibb on behalf of the Warrington creditors. The amount received for this " after acquired" property , at Hull. alter deducting all expenses, was .037 14s, This allows a supplemental bli-vidend of 7s 104 d in the £ to be paid to Warrington creditors on debts amounting to .£1,333 19s 7d. In refer- ring to this matter, the Official Receiver regrets that the amount of the supplemental divi- dend is not so large as originally anticipated. Un- fortunately rebates, to a considerable amount. bad to be made to the purchasers of the timber sold lsy Public auction, in accordance with the conditions !If sale, a great part thereof, when unstacked, prov- ing to be of a quality much inferior to that which had been advertised. It will be seen that the Warrington creditors have received a total dividend of los id in the -C on their accounts.</p>
      </div>
