      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>BIRD SHOW AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>BIRD SHOW AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A Successful Event.</p>
        <p>The s-iventh annual show of cansries, mules, British and foreign birds, and pigeons, promoted by the Warrington and District Ornithological Society was held in the Conservative Club Assembly Room, on Saturday, and was attended with gratifying success.. The show was larger than any of its pre- decessors and the exhibits were of a very high order. The entries in the pigeon claszes numbered 257, being an increase of about forty on last year. The open classes were well filled and some very fine birds were shown.Mr E Burrows was a very succes- ful local exhibitor. In the cage bird classes the entries were about fifty in excess of last year. Com- petition was very keen, birds of exceptionally high quality being exhibited. The Norwich plainheads were a splendid feature, Mr J A Eckersley, of Edge- baston, Birmingham, Mr W Booth, of Hyde, Mr W Dowth waite, of Lancaster, and Mr If Barker, of Cuerdley, being the principal winners. Two very rare specimens were shown by Messrs Williams and Browning, of Liverpool, one being a canary. bullfinch and the other a linnet-bullfinch. Both are considered very valuable. Some very choice foreign birds belonging to Mr Osbaldeston, of Preston, attracted considerable attention. The members classes were very creditable. The judges, who dis- charged their duties with satisfaction to all, were :—Canaries, Mr A G Davies, Manchester ; British birds and mules, Mr C Houlttn, St Helens pigeons, Mr H A Moore, Lymm. The affiliate of the society to whom praise is due for the excellent arrangements. are: chairman, Mr T Mather; vice-chairman, Mr J D Leigh ; committee, Messrs Hampson, Barker, Bur- rows, Bray, Appleton, Lowe, Houghton, Cook and Booth ; treasurer, Mr G Wright ; secretary, Mr J D Leigh. The show was opened by Councillor Tinnion who delivered an appropriate speech, Mr T Mather presiding. A. hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Councillor Tinnion on the proposition of Mr J D Leigh seconded by Mr Hampson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>PRIZE LIST</head>
        <p>THE PRIZE LIST</p>
        <p>The principal prize winners were:-Pigeens (open classes), -Working homer (cock), bred prior to 1902, likeliest for fly- ing purposes : 1, B Burrows, Warrington; 2, J Cooke, Sandbach ; 3, D Shakeshaft. Lymm ; extra 3, T Fisher, Bolton-le-Sands. Working homer (hen), bred prior to 1902, likeliest for flying purposes : 1.3 13 Billiagton,Southport; 2. Burrows. Warrington; 3, Dr 3 Wright, Lymrn, extra 3, D Shakeshaft, Working homer (cock), bred in 1902, likeliest for Eying purposes : 1 and 3, B Burrows, Warrington; 2. J Webster, Prestwich ; extra 3, T Fisher. Working homer (hen), bred in 1912, likeliest for flying purposes : 1, F Andrews, Leigh ; 2, B Burrows. Warrington . • 3, T Pigott, Lymm ; extra 3. D Shakeshaft, Lynam. Working homer (cock), bred in 1902, that has flown 100 miles or over : 1, B H Dennis, Northwicla ; 2, J Lythgoe, Warrington ; 3. F Bloor, Warrington; extra 3, B Burrows. Warrington. Work- ing homer (hew, bred in 1902, that has flown 100 miles or over: 1 and 2, B Burrows, Warrington ; 3, W W Leigh. War- rington ; extra 3, W Blundell, Southport. Any age (cock or hen) working homer, not haring Pearl or white eyes, 1 and 2. B Burrows ;3. Sayce Bros, New Ferry ; extra 3, V Bioor. Cagebirds (open classes).-Yorkshire yellow cock clear. ticked or unevenly marked: 1, W Silcock, Lithetland ; 2. j Gibbons, Old Trafford; 3, C Boote, Runcorn. Yorkshire buff cock. clear, ticked or unevenly marked: 1. F Spencer, St Helens; 2 and 3,F Ditehfield. Runcorn. Yorkshire hen, yellow or buff clear, ticked or unevenly marked: 1 and 3, G H Hatt:bone : 2. J F Gibbons. Old Trafford. Norwich yellow cock, clear : 1, W Do wthwaite Lancaster; 2, R Barker. Cnerdley ; 3, W Booth, Hyde. Norwich buff cock, clear: 1, W Dowthwaite, Lancaster; 2 B 11 Godwin,Penketh; 3. J. Brothertoa. Bolton. Norwich yellow cock, ticked or unevenly marked: 1, W Booth, Hyde; 2. R Baker; 3, J Leadbetter, Present. Norwich buff cock, ticked or unevenly marked: 1 and 2. 1Z Barker; 3, W Dowthwaite, Lancaster. Norwich yellow hen, clear ticked or unevenly marked: 1, A Eckersley, Birmingham ; 2 and 3. Et Baker. Norwich I buff hen, clear, ticked or unevenly marked : 1 J. A Bakers- ley. Birmingham ; 2, J P Ball. St Helens, 3, I A Mather. Widnes. Norwich yellow unflighted clear. ticked or unevenly marked : 1. J A Bckersley,l3irmingbam 2, W Booth, Hyde ; 3, U Barker. Norwich buff, untlighted clear, ticked, or unevenly marked: 1, 3 A Eckersley, Birmingham ; 2, B R Godwin, Penketh ; 3 3 Leadbetter, Preseot. Selling class (125 6d including cage) : 1, Hampton • 2, Wootton and Pitchford, Stoke-on-Trout; 3, W. I Bray, Ba;lestown. Under the patronage of the National I British Bird and Mule Club, London.-Mule any variety (light) 1. Williams and Browning, Liverpool ; 2. J P Ball, N BB and M. 0.: 3. Williams and Browning. Mule any variety (dark) 1, Williams and Browning. Liverpool; 2 and 3. J P Ball Hybrid, any variety between two Britist• birds: 1 and 2. Williams and Browning; 33 P Ball. British Goldfinch : 1, J P Ball; 2. Wootton and Pitchford, Stoke-on-Trent ; 3, It V Raine. N•B.B. and M.C. Any other variety, hard bill or seed eaters : 1, R V Baine •, 2, J P Ball; 3,3 Lowe. Thrush. blackbird or starling : 1 and 2. F C Sharp. Bariestown ; 3, J T Mills, St Helens. Foreign (large). single or in pairs ; 1,2, and 3, W Osbaldeston, Preston. Foreign (small), single or in pairs 1, 2. and 3. W Osbaideston. Gift class (birds to be sold for the society's benefit) : 1, B It Godwin; 2, J D Leigh, Warrington; 3. G Billington, Warrington. Members' classes.-Yorkshire yellow cock, clear, ticked or unevenly marked : 1 and 2, B Houghton; 3, W Bray. York- shire buff cock. clear, ticked or unevenly marked : land 2, 11 Houghton ; 3, 3 Hatupson. Yorkshire yellow hen, clear, ticked or unevenly marked 1 and 2,W Bray; 3, B Health- ton. Yorkshire buff hen, clear, ticked or unevenly marked s 1, 11 Houghton ; 2, J Hampson; 3, W Bray, Norwich yellow cock, clear, ticked or unevenly marked : 1 and 2, R Barker; 3, J Hampson. Norwich buff cock, clear, ticked or unevenly marked 1 and 2, B Barker ; 3, J D Leigh, Norwich yellow or buff hen, clear, ticked or unevenly marked 1 and 2, B Barker ;3. 3 D Leigh. Norwich Crest: 1, 3 D Leigh • 2. 3 Appleton ; 3 H Tyrer. Norwich crest bred : 1, D Leigh ;2, fi Tyrer ; 3, R Barker. Green canary of any variety 1 and 2. J Hampson; 3, 3 D Leigh. Any variety at previously mentioned : 1,2, and 3. J Hampson. Light mule 1 and 3, 3 Hampson; 2; 3 D Leigh. Dark mule: 1.3 D Leigh ;2, G Billington ;3, 3 MIMPSOII. British goldfinch or bullfinch: 1, J Hampson: 2, F C Sharp; 3, 3 Lowe Brown linnet a 1 B Houghton: 2, 3 Bamplon ; 3, J Davies, Siskin, redpoll or twite : 1 and 3, J D Leigh; 2, J Lowe. Any other British bard bill. not larger than a hawfinch ; 1, R Barker; 2, F C Sharp ; 3, J Lowe. British soft bill, not larger than a thrush : 1. G Wright; 2. Bowers ; 3. V 0 Sharp. Any variety foreign, single or in pairs : 1, B Barker ; 2, 3 Hampson ' • 3 J Lowe. Selling class (bird and cage, 101; 6d) : 1 and 3. J Hampson ; 2. W Bray: Selling class (pairs. 214) : 1, ft Barker ; 2, W Bray ; 3. H Tvrer. Novice classes.-Yorkshire yellow cock, clear, ticked or variegated : 1, H C Bingham, Sankey Bridges; 2. G Hubble; H 0 Bingham. Yorkshire buff cock, clear, ticked or varie- gated • 1 and 2, 3 Shaw; 3, J Sherwood. Yorkshire hen clear, ticked or variegated 1 and 3, 3 Skew ; 2.1 H C Bingham. Yorkshire cock or hen, natural colour, ticked or variegated : 1. H C Bingham ; 2,3 Sherwood; 3, Bowers. Norwich buff cock, clear, ticked, or variegated : land 3. J Davies ; 2, C Hargreaves. Mule of any variety: 1, J Sher- wood ; 2. Bowers ;3, J Davies. British goldfinch 1. 3 Davies ; 2. Bowers. Hyde's special (for best bird shown by a novice), H 0 Bingham. F Caperns prizs (Bristol), for the best goldfinch; 3 Hampsnn.</p>
        <p>A Steamer Damaged at Latchford. The steamer "Syran Prince" was passing through Latchford Locks on her way to Liverpool on Tuesday when she struck the locks and was damaged.</p>
