      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>?Wm's to the Etitton _ Thelwall Macintyre</head>
        <p>?Wm's to the Etitton _ Thelwall Macintyre</p>
        <p>and the Council</p>
        <p>Sir,—la your issue of the Bth Mr Maclntyre is reported to have said at a meeting of the Runcorn Rural District , Council respecting a statement made by me in my com- plaint to the Cheshire County Council This was an unmitigated—he would not say a lie, but it was far from the truth."—" e would as soon pledge the wind as pledge a Council of over 40 high-spirited gentlemen." I also see that Mr Woodward, on the behalf of the Council, testified their confidence in Mr Maclntyre. Please allow me space therefore in your next issue to tell Mr Maclntyre a... 3 the rest of the " high-sp.rited gentiamth " that the expression of confidence should be at once withdrawn, for I can prove not only what I said in my statement about Mr Maclntyre but every other statement I have made in connection with this public scandal of gross neglect of duty by these "high-spirited gentlemen " and their officials. Lot these brave men show a little genuine ap.r.t and hero they have a grand chance for I shall be glad to appear before the Council and bring five witnesses who will state in the most:positive manner that MrMacktyre the active agtht .of the tannery property, did malts to' statement which I repeat in my complaint to the Chif - hire County Council. I shall hope to give Mr Maclntyre and his " high spirited " associates several opportuni- ties of displaying their courage, and when it comes to a question of " truth I nave go )d reason to bops shall win the encounter. .</p>
        <p>.._,_ ... LA the Rural District Council keep up their spirits for they will want them when they have to face the mus:c. Yours truly,</p>
        <p>R. A. NAYLOR, Sr. Mi3bce 's Home, EvrtoV, irdms, t+. W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Education Bill.</head>
        <p>Education Bill.</p>
        <p>Mr. Balfour and the Nonconformists.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt is one of the strange phenomena of the Eluca- tien controverli that a man of such acute intellect as Mr Balfour should utterly fail to understand and to apron- ciate the position of the Nmeonformist.s in opposing this Bill. In his famous speech in Manchester, be said "If the Nonconformists really and genuinely wanted their grievances 'remedied, would not they ball with acclamation a Bill which dealt with so many of the real difficulties under which they labour P Would they not hail which acclamation a Bill which enables them for the first time in history to build,out of pubic funds, a school conducted according to their own views, I or it may be prejudices, even in places where the neces- sary accommodation ia provided by f °hod/ be'oeging:to another denomination?" ' Now in the first place, it would be difficult to find words which would be I more misleading regarding the wording and sense of clause nine of the Bill. In his desire to create a favourable view of hi; endeavours to remedy what he calls Nonconformist grievances, he has allowed himself to use language which altggether misrepresents I the intention of this clause. Ninety nine persons out of a hundred reading the above would consider that Mr Balfour had provided in the Bill for the building, out of public fund;, Nonconformist Nhools which should be conducted according to their own views and prejudices nee. Now everyone, and Mr Balfour be it of all, knows that clause nine of the Bill provides nothing of the sort. What it does pr wide is,that the Educational Authority can erect with the public funds public achools which can be popularly managed. These public schools can no more be conducted according to the views and pre- judices of Nonconformists than they can be conducted according to the views and prejudices of churchmen. They are citizen school; cede', that can be built out of nubile funds and rightly so. But in respect to schools which Nonconformists may be permitted to build at their , own expense, Mr Balfour knows well that the positien of every Nonconformist Church in erg ire to education, is that education can only be effectively undertaken by the State. That inae- much as the State, that is the citizens of the State, pay for the education, then the citizens shoold manage it , and that there should be no divorce between the financial responsibility and the administrative control. A measure which invites Nonconformists to abandon their principles and encroach upon the province of the State by erecting more sectarian schools, is not one , which we should be asked to hail with acclamation as a remedy for an acknewledged grievance, and I trust that Nonconformists will decline to abandon their prin- ciples and enter into competition with the church in the erection of more sectarian schoole which would in the future increase the difficulty of attaining a thoroughly national system. But let us for ain nnent put aside the principles of the Free Churches and:consider further the practical bearing of Clause Nine as a remedy for the grievance. The application for the erection of a new school is to be decided by the educstional department on three grounds. The interests of secular instruction, the wishes of the parents, and the question of the rates. We may take as an instance ia village school of which there are not a few, in which the controlling religious influ- ence and atmosphere is High Church and differing little from Boman Catholic. Free churchmen and all others ought to be protected from being nompPlled by the Stets to send their children to a school where the re- ligious teaching is repugnant to them. The State proposes through Mr Balfour's Bill to find a remedy by asking the Nonconformists to build a school of their own, not out of public funds as Mr Balfour says, but at their own expense, which when built can be run at the expense of the State. But naturally the ratepayers will object to have to run two school; when, as Balfour says, the necessary accommodation is pro- vided by a school belonging to another denomination, and the Department must decide. On what principle will it sdjudicate ? It has to settle rival claims, but the claims are of an entirely different character, the one is of a moral character, and the other financial. The one has its origin in the innermost sanctuary of a man's being, the other is a question of rates. How is the one claim to be measured and weighed against the other ? You might as well try to measure a thought with a foot rule, as to try and measure the one againet the other. If the claim be settled on moral grounds, then the ratepayers will have a grievance of which they will be reminded every time they have the rate to pay. Instead of visiting this open the Government who created the absurd cmiition, they will visit it upon those who took the only course open to them of withdrawing their children from an atmosphere which in their opinion was permeated with what they believe to be untrue. If on the other hand the pockets of the ratepayers prevail, and the application of the section of parents is declined, then the wrong to them and to their children is continued. The Bill lands us in many absurd position;, and the reason for this doe; not arise from any lack of desire on the part of Mr Balfour to deal fairly with rain the working ant of his plan. The difficulties all arise from trying to adjust two differ- ent and often opposing ideals, the ideal of the Church and the ideal of the State. Mr Balfour is like a mariner who tries to steer his bark, in this case the Education Bill, by two compasses, which cannot agree together where the North Pole is. First he obeys one and then the other, and so we have a zig-zag course which pleases nobody and which may ultimately lead to the foundering of the ship. Take the cage of the question of the appointment of the teachers, bead master, assistants, and pupil teachers, or perhaps better still the Kenyon-Slaney amendment. Mr Balfour starts with the intention of reserving for the denomination the undisputed control of the religious edutatien, and strives to adjuattthe principle with some sort of elective control. Now in dealing with the establishment, if this Church is to have control of the religious instruction in its own school; it should be free to exercise it in a^cmdance with its own constitutien. All religious instruction, as the Rev Mr Carleton sbewe, is invested la the c'ergy controlled by the Bishops, and if the Establishment is to have the Con- trol of the religious teaching in echo ols then it should be exercised only through those channels accredited by the Church. Were Ito view the question from the standpoint of a Church clergyman, I should be disposed to take exactly the stand Mr Carleton does. But then this conflict,' with the idea of management be any kind of elected or Selected body, it lands as back to the " one man management which conflicts with the State ideal, and so we have the Kenyon-Staney amend- ment accepted, which expressly states that the religious Instruction must be in the hands of managers as a whole and therefere not in the hands of the clergy, and se Mr Balfour is paying the penalte of these who try to please everyone. Churchmen and Roman Catholics are begin- ning to ask, is the Bill worth having? And whether after all the elaborate scheme for preserving the de- nominational schools, and at the same time trying to fit them into a national system, will not pave the way to their destruction CHARLES E. PARKER. Penketh, November 12, 1902,</p>
        <p>The " Queen" Newspaper has,for the last thirty years, been prominent in putting before tbe +public grime of the very best specimens of coloured printed pictures, on which no expense or trouble has been spared. This year, quite a new departure from the lices which have hitherto found such favour has been made. With the Christmas number will be presented a handsome work of art in the form of a Rembrandt Gravure 22 x 30 inches. It will be a reproduction of the painting by I Snowman, exhibited in this year's Royal Academy, and favourably noticed, entitled " Confidences." It is a charming picture of social domestic life, representing two young ladies in a picturesque, old Eoglish garden. The younger of the two, little more than a child, kneel- ing at the side of the ether figure, has handed to her elder sister what is Pvidently her first love letter which, while being read, provokes a smile. The pic;ure which, an already mentioned, wan most favourably noticed during its exhibition at Burlington House, is artistically producld on proof paper, and as a real work of art is worth many times the sum charged. The " Queen'' newspaper will as usual be replete with most interesting matter, appropriate to the season of Christmas, and contributed by titled ladies well hown in society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIPAY NOVEMBER 14, 1902</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIPAY NOVEMBER 14, 1902</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ' ATHLETICS. --- Rugby Football. ['By Ovalist.] Warringtoa have once more actied the scalp of Old ham to the season's trophies, though they only won on Saturday by a solitary point after a hard game of a- not very scientific character. However, a victory over Oldham is always a tit-bit and the margin of points is not a thing to grumble about when success has been obtained.</p>
        <p>There was a good crowd considering the stormy weather, and Oldham brought the. usual couple of hundred supporters and a practically full team. War- rington were handicapped by the absence of Dickenson who was found guilty of a breach of the professional laws the previous evening. The vacancy was filled by Edmunds. Oldham won the toss and taking advantage of a strong breeze they soon made.their presence felt near the Warrington line. The Oldham of two years ago would have scored very soon, but the team of to-day is nothing like so formithble, and Warrington easily kept out the would- be try-zetters. Harris twice led play into the Oldham half, but the cherry-and-whites were soon back. Just about quarter-time history repeated itself,for Fish inter- cepted an Oldham pass in exactly the same place as he did last year, and ran three-parts the length of the field. He had Thomas to dodge this time, however, and though he easily beat the full-back he lost ground and was pulled down by Williams. Even then he threw to Burgess, who had a walk-in, but he stumbled and finally fell one yard short of the line. It was an excit- ing couple of minutes and Warrington quite deserved the points they did not get. Oldham carried the suc- ceeding scrimmage and got down to the home quarter once more, S Lees, jun , dropping a neat goal just before the interval.</p>
        <p>Oldham thus turned round leading by two points to nil, but there was not much hope of their ulimate success. A Spindled= committee-man on the stand remarked that Warrington would put on ten points, and they ought to have done but bad play lost many chances. In the first two minutes however, the backs got going and Isherwood served Fish, who made a brilliant dash down the touch-line and scored far out, the Oldham men having no chance with him. The scorer took the goal-kick and was as near success as possible without actually "doing the Trick." Though beaten forward Warrington continued to have the better of the play and smarter combination would have gained further points. Perhaps the wind was the cause, but anyhow the halves did an unusual quantity of mulling. Oldham also w ,sted opportunities and ultimately had to retire beaten by three points to two points.</p>
        <p>The game was the poorest I ever saw between War- rington and Oldham, and pretty well disgusted the crowd, who expected something tasty from such a classic fixture. The number of penalties against the halves and forwards on both sides quite spoiled the game, while Warrington's weakness in the pack made matters worse. Oldham had double the opportunities vouchsafed Warrington but never looked like getting a try, whereas the home backs were always dangerous 1 when they got going. Though they played it badly Oldham stuck to the open game and went in very little for the spoiling tactics one generally sees adopted against Warrington. Fish is generally hemmed in,but on Saturday he had only his:vis-a-vis to face and conse- quently won the match single handed. The Oldham men are still speedylbut they lack a man like T. D. Davies, Fletcher or Sam Lees in the centre, where Thomas and Civil seem strong but rather slow. In feet Civil on Saturday earned the severe criticism from an Oldham supporter that he was "too slow to carry dinners." Certainly he was a long way below form. Williams and Spottswood both ran well on the wing but were well looked after. Harris, however, let his man get past once, and the result might have been serious near the line. The Oldham halves, A Lees and S Lees, jun., were moderate, Dick Thomas at full back being by far the best man in the team. lis display both with the wind and against it was splendid. The forwards also did their share, as the fact that they got the ball nineteen times out of the last twenty-one scrummages. shows. Warrington were badly whipped in this department.</p>
        <p>Isherwood and Fish took the honours on the home side, the remaining backs only playing a moderate game. So far, Warrington have played all their best games away from home, and the spectators have never yet seen them in form. This was often the case last season, when they did well on foreign soil.</p>
        <p>The scarcity of home engagements so far has re- duced the club exchequer very considerably, and at the present time the takings are £2OO less than they were this time last year Expenses are quite as high, of course. "There's a good time coming," let us hope.</p>
        <p>Dickenson was sadly miss,,d on Saturday, for tricky as Edmunds is, he cannot get over the ground like Warrington's lengthy young centre. Unfortunately we shall have to do without Dickenson for another month as the Northern Union Committee on Tuesday evening suspended him up to and including December 13, when Warrington play Widnes at Widnes. The intervening matches are Broughton Rangers, Hull Kingston Rovers and Hunslet away, and Wigan at home, BO that the team have some hard work to do during his absence. The penalty inflicted is just about as much as was ex- pected for the punishment is rarely light for however slight a breach of the professional laws. It showed how unimportant the committee thought the offence was when they merely fined the Club the nominal sum of lOs 6d. I hope the incident will be a warning to the other players for the loss of a " quarter " is a more serious matter than they, perhaps, believed, though they have been told often enough.</p>
        <p>This unfortunate suspension further weakens the Warrington team for the engagement with the Rangers at Broughton and they will have all their work cut out to get the better of the Wheater's Field lot, reserves though most of them are. Warrington are very badly off for substitutes and will not be much stronger,minus Fish and Dickenson, than the folio wing fifteen selected to represent the Rangers. Fielding ; Eckersall, Barrett, Widdeson, and Hampson ; Driver and Findlow ; White- head, Winskill, Beetham, Scott, Warwick, Garretty, Hardisty and Atkinson. Reserve: Locke.</p>
        <p>Wilson, Hog g, Sam James, W James, and Oram have all been picked to play against Yorkshire .while F Harry is on the injured list. This is undoubtedly hard on the Rangers, who have reason to growl at County fixtures. It will be noticed also that to-day Bradford, Swinton, Salford, and Hunslet are without games. Who- ever is to blame for so ar-anging the fixtures as to make this ridiculous clashing ought to be told some home truths.</p>
        <p>From a Warrington point of view the question is : Flow to win at Broughton? A lot will again depend on the work of the forwards but I• am glad to see that the Committee have decided to play Harris on Isherwood's wing. There wi I this be one scoring wing, whereas if Harris and Isherwoodremainori in their present positions the three- quarter line w ,uld be just as disjointed and ineffective as it was at Salford, when Dickenson and Fish were absent. The value of having the best men together was proved by the way Burgess, Hockenhall, Isher- wood, and Dickenson won the Leigh match with com• bination alone.</p>
        <p>Runcorn have got into grave trouble over the Halifax match on Saturday, when the Linnets had to bow the knee, after, it is alleged, scoring tries which were unfairly disallowed. The amount of feeling shown was evidently greater than originally pictured. According to the referee, Mr Clegg, of Manningham, the crowd assaulted him, the Halifax secretary, and some of the Halifax players.</p>
        <p>The matter has been reported to the Northern Union, the referee stating that he was mobbed by hundreds of people, struck on the side of the jaw, and kicked on the leg.:One Halifax player, it is allegeoi, was wounded on ;the head by a stone wrapped in paper. These statements are denied at Runcorn, from whence on Runcorn correspondent sends us some impressions See "Runcorn Notes."</p>
        <p>The South-West Lancashire League up to date is a follows Points</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. For At Pt. Warrington Leigh Runcorn Wigsn St. Helens Widnes</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>A ST. HELENS COMPENSATION _CLAIM.</p>
        <p>At the St. Helens County Court on Wednesday, Mark Cooper, pettier, claimed ( ompensation from the Sutton Reath Colliery Company in respect of an accident. The plaintiff had come to an ;agreement with the Colliery Company that he should receive 10s a week, but after— wards found he was entitled to 17g., and now asked to have the agreement revised. The defendants relied upon the agreement, which, however, was unstamped. Judge Shand said he wasaurprised at a colliery company doing a thing of this kind. It was scandalous. Be supposed it must be some wretched insurance company. It was stated that the Company were only insured for fatal accidents and not for injuries and would have to pay this themselves. The Judae held that the agreeme eat being made in duress was not binding upon the appii cant, whom he awarded 1s ad a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>wARRIN3I ON NOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATION. ---- Meeting at the Liberal Club. ---- Speeches by Mr. A H. Crosfield and Miss Robinson.</head>
        <p>wARRIN3I ON NOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATION. ---- Meeting at the Liberal Club. ---- Speeches by Mr. A H. Crosfield and Miss Robinson.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Liberal Club on Monday evening,. the anembers of the Women's Liberal Association bell their annual tea and meeting. Tea was served in the committee room upstairs, and subsequently, the busi- ness meeting was held In the reading nem. The pro. ceedings were presided over by Mr A Fl Crosfield, and amongst those nresent were : —Mrs Zitia A-mitage (nrre'dent), Mrs Walker (bon. treasurer), Mrs C Jaekson Holmes (bon. secretary), Miss E len Robinson. of Liver- pool, Miss Dutton, Mrs Clay, Mrs Kisby Mrs Wyllie,and , obey. The speeches were agreeably interspersed with several vocal and instrumental selections. Mrs Holmes commenced the proceedings by a pianoforte solo,played with much taste and skill, and Miss Mabel Efenshall afterwards delighted the company with a solo in which her sweet soprano voice was heard to advantage. Miss Kathleen Merchant- ,of Liverpool, also sang tw s olos ' 'during the evening, and fully maintained the high reputation she gained at a recent concert in Parr Hall. Miss Bertha Bouchier was warmly applauded for a , violin sob which was given with m irked ability and characterised by a delicacy of treatment. The piAnists during the evening were Mr Dicks, of Liverpool, and Mrs Jackson 11-,lrries, MR. CHOSFIELD'S</p>
        <p>The Cnaltinten, who was very cordiely receive I,sai,l : I.; is a happy thought to flavour our politics with a little Music in this way. Shall I put it that we have se it were, added an artistic gilding to the political pl,l which you will now have to swallow. (Laughter) It is rather remarkable that we have such a good gather- ing, because after the keen and indeed intenee interest which our municipel contests have excited—orei the more attention they eet from the public and the hotter at all times—it might have seemcd a little dull to you to turn from highly important enunieip %l matters to mere minor matters of Imperial policy. (Laughter.) As it happens when I was speskine at thie ash shortly before the elections, amongst others our friends Mr Upson and Mr Brophy as well as CounciPer Tlenien were preeent. I took the opportunity of wishing theel all SUCCO99 and I think the Liberal party has done uncommonly well in theee elections and 'strengthened our position in the Council. Turnine to Imp,ri ques- tions, &amp;nee I last had the privilege of addressing veu there have been two events of great impertance in the country. The terrible and tragic war in South Africa hag been brought to a clege, and there has been another 'ovent for which we are all deeply thankful, the King's life has been spared to us aee lend time after a d sneer- oue inners. (Bear hear.) By his kind and gnial nature, his dignity, his unfailing tact and insight into eff ,irs, and let US not forget also the energy with which he undertakes so many duties, the King has won equally the respect and affection of the natien,and his courage and unselfishness at a very trying time heve "lied a deeper note of sympathy. (Hear,hear.) I think we all recoenise that in our constitution, the Sovereign is a great clesl more than a!me.re figurehead. The Sloth African war heeled to much diviesion of opinion of late yeers,hnt no one point we are all animal at p-eiene. We wish Mr nhemberlain well in his investigations on the spot, and we hope that his efforts in South Afriea may to some extent help to heal the wounds and erase the bitter memories that are left in the Transvaal and the Free State. The division of niiinion on South African affairs, to which I have just referred, has by nn means run on mere party lines. Take for Instance our clue) here. I keow tloat a large proportion of our members leek upon this war as having been inevitable and necessary, and therefore, a just war. We have respected their opinions. and I honestly think these who hold that convietion are to be envied, and I for one appreriate very mirth the courtesy with which one has been listened to in this cluh,in arguing the contrary. lam not going to weary you with a repetition of what I ventured to discuss at length in our Warrington newenepere. If I briefly summarize one or two points it is Cr elear ma a mis- conception one still finds cropping up now and then. The misconception is Chic, that those of us who centen- dad that no case was ever made out for this war as either inevitable, necessary, or jest, were nreosred Cs I knuckle under to President Kruger and other Terles in the Trenevaal,whe at fi-st were reluctant enough Co I make the concessions demanded Flow entirely this is a misconception is shown by Mr Chamberlain' admis- sion that NINE-TENIRS OF OUR DEXANDI HAD BEEN CONCEDED.</p>
        <p>In private life if two people quarrel, and then try 'n strike a bargain, and Ode of them gets ninr-tenths of what be appears to be asking, he usually thinks he is doing pretty well. (Laughter.) Remember this Was not a remark let fall in debate in the House of Commons, or any mere figure of speech. It wss a deliberate state- ment in a despatch sent by Mr Chamberlain to the Governor of Natal, and what We ask is. " Why when such concessions were made, did We. by Our evil diplomacy, prod the Boers into ',su- ing their desperate ultimatum r, In my opinion the answer to that cal be given and only given by the same unscrupulous set of mon who, having first organised the Jameson raid, were not content until they had brought about this war into the harrain. If we reoall the past few years is there a single man in Parliament or in authority who would not gladly make terms:on the basis of nine-tenths? yes or for the matter of that three-fourths of our original demands, and Eruct to the largely increased numbers, the wealth and the Mu- the Outlanders, to bringiabout the Federation of South Africa in the inevitable nrogreas of civlization ? (Applause). Fifty years ago John Bright was turned out of Manchester for condemning the Crimean Wit,. They bustled and spat upon him in the etreet. Halt a century later the Tory Premier toll us that at that time we had put our mooey on the wrong ha,qe. Well wisdom of that kind 'which COM.P.SO long after the eveot, seems wretchedly cheap unless it teaches us to be more level- headed in the future. In our own time the hest brains of the countrymen of the widest political knowledge and insight, such men as Herbert Spencer, James Ern, and John Morley, have seen plainly enough the sort of forees at work in South Africa. These men and others have not hesitated to speak out boldly and sternly, and they deserve the support, I think, of every Liberal who, in his heart, detests the evil course which Mr Chamberlain , pursued in that fatal summer of 1839. This war was to have cost us ten millions ; the latest estimate. I think, is two hundred and twenty-three minima. Thanks to the genius and energy of Lord Kitchener, to the fine combination of tenacity and patience with couragc, shown by our troops, we were fortunately victors. But again I say, COUlfl WA retrace our steps, is there a man in hie Senses who would allow the Colonial Seer ntary and Lord Milner to pursue a course of diplomacy which Was less worthy of statesmen than of some Birmingham wirepuller playing to the gallery ? (Hear, hear.) I have spoken of the c ntt to us in blood and treasure; think of the cost to those two countries. Near a century since Sidney Smith, a Liberal Churchmao,—we still have some broad-minded Liberal Churchmen now to leaven the others —(laughter) —aid one of them has just left Warrington, where I am sorry to Ray be was much wanted and will be badly miased—(applause)—wrote as follows If three men were to have their legs and arms broken and were to remain all night exposed to the inclemency of the weather' the whole country would be in a state of most frightful agitation Look at the wholesale deaths on a field of battle, ten acres covered with dead and half dead and dying, and the shrieks and agonies of many th mends of human beings. There is more of misery infl eted upon mankind by one year of war than by All the civil peculation,' in a century." If that is true of an ordinary conflict, think of the conaermeneea. the pr-- longed mental and physical agony, the equalid horrors of disease and death which follow when it is impossible, not to win a battle remember, not to overthrow a Gove-nment, but to extinguish a nationality. except by ' starving out the country. (Hear, hear.) Besides the soldiers who have fallen fighting our battle—and the tighter the place we found ourselves in the greater the honour to them for getting us nut of it—the oost of this war we may put at £223,000,000. It has wined out the savings effected by our statesmen for the last thirty year'. It mean,' a huge addition to the national debt, but be- sick s this our annual expenditure far thelaet graven years has advanced at the rate of five and a half millions a year. That is to say. it is pretty nearly forty millions a year more new than it was in 1896 You would ;ay that expenditure on this colossal scale should be vigilantly scrutinized, and yet it is not very long since our over- worked House of Commons voted sixty-seven millions in a single day. IS THERE NO REMEDY FOR THIS SORT OF y</p>
        <p>_ There is the plain and simple remedy ofidevolution, whether it be some Rule all round or whether taring one thing at a time we begin with a proper mount's of self-government, under control of the Im- perisl Parliament, for Ireland. The nation will not lis'en to this yet awhile, but we sha'l have to get rid of some of our ignorant prejudices on the mitt., FO ,ner or Islet in our own interests, and I think it wid be sooner rather than later. (Rear, hear.) Meantime there is another cons&lt;quence of our increased expenditure,which will b. felt by everyone if there is any truth in the remark that a man's most sensitive place is hia pocket. (Laughter.) Besides piling up a debt which is a handi- cap in all our commercial competition with our enter- prising cousins in America. we have had to begin increasing taxation at home The little trifling formality of payment—(taughter)—bad, after all, to he faced, and the question was how to raise the money. The Govern- ment, as you know, began increasing the taristion on the necessaries of life. It is argued that if the democracy enjoys a little" Maffiching" they should help to nay for it. There is a good deal in this, and I freely admit so long as we fit in with Hurley's description of man as a fighting animal it will be dangerous enough to It people get into the way of looking at foreign wars as we watch a play at a theatre. The cost and consequences must be brought home to us, hut I refuse altogether to admit that that is the slightest justification for this taxation of flour of which we have heard so much. There are twenty things that should be taxed rather than bread. That is the very last thing that should be taxed, for the</p>
        <p>simple reason that it presses most on those classes of the community who of all people can least aff rd to pay it. (Hear, hear.) You kale.' that, thanks to the lalemrs of Mr Charles Bloch in Leaden, and the inveati gattoas made by Mr Re syntree in York, it has been celculated that fully a quarter of the whole popu'ation of this country are insufficiently fed. That is to say, many millions of our people have to struggle on w:t lout enough food to enable teem to do a decent day's work, to sey nothing of the leisure, pleasure, or culture which make life so much better worth living. We know that hundreds of thousands of these people are as it were on the brink of the Social abyss wnich Mr Booth has des- •ebed as "the submerged tenth." Toe eft ct of this tax, which has as a rule increased the eerie of the loaf by id., and where it has not done So, has depreciated the quality of the bread,will be to push poor people ever the edge. rhis tax is not only a cruel tax, but it ie wasteful as well, bemuse of 43 paid by the consumer 14 oely reaches the Government. It has been calculated that every man earning a pound a week has his cost of living raised by this protective policy of the Government which practically therefore LOWERS WAGES BY 6D TO is a WEEK. (Hear, hear.) We have heard much in these days of patriotism, and most of it one is happy to think is the genuine article. Sorely patriotism, like charity, begies at home. The United Kingdom with its force millions is after all,the heart and core of the Empire of which we are all so proud. The Eitheire will be strengthened and the happiness and prosperity of E 'gland increased not by devitalizing millions of our people, lowering their capacity an i standard of life, but sy raising all three. People say the money must be found somehow, and excuse the tax on that plea, as if there were not a tar greater source of rovenue to our hand in the t ixstion of teed values and of site values,weice may be taken with- out any hardship and with absolute justice. Broadly speaking you know that as the p mutation in our great centres of industry increases, so the value of land rise. Improvements in streets, in supplies of weter, gas, and means of locomotion, ii the laying out of p rice, or in • improved drainage, all these uniertskines eerried out at the public expense further increase and have in no end of cases already intreased the value of land am -st beyond the dreams of avarice. Is it not extraordinary that in a demecratio counrry like ours these land values in cities should not be taxed both for local and Imperial purposes ? As to the texatien of site values, what that simply means is this. In the neighboer clod or large towns rou have land rated at agricultural value, and if I am not mistaken half the rates art often paid now f r the landlords under the oeeretion of the dole given them a few years since. It,thi rsfor emay,and often does,paye, landlord to keep that land unoccupied in close prcx miter to a town or city, and se to ClO,O an overcrowding in every way injurious to our population. /leer, heir).The remedy is very simple. Toe landlord should tie tax ti on the land at the value he himself put; upon it. It will then no longer pay him to keep the lend unoccupied and—l am only stating the matter vrre broadly—you will at crece Bud it easier to get wide roads, open spaces, rapid means of access to and from wrrk, which is an absolute necessity for work people, and in the eurroundings of a town a development of these city gariens, which above all thing,e will most help to solve what Carlyle edited the "condition of &amp;intends groblarn. (Applause). With the possible exception of education alone, there is nothing in the whole field of poliics ria which the future welfare of them in, we -nen, amid children of this country depends so much es on this reform in TAXATION GP GROUND RENTS AND SITE VALUES Nor is tire anything which will by increasing the 1 healthy selrerespect of people indirectly do se much to 1 further the great cause of temperance. (Applause). So when you hear of these things or the comparative value of such indirect taxation as this flour tax, or the splendid measure of direct taxation carried by Sir William Harcourt, the death duties—(cheers)-11 no; look upon them as dry topics which have little practical interest or bearing on the well-being of our townsfolk. They have a most vital arid direct bearing upon it. It is, we all know, very easy in fastidieue and cynical moment—end very cheap—to cavil at politics. Those who meddle with them have to make up their minds to what Lord Rosebiry the other day called " the traffie in expediency." And then we are familiar with what is called " cupboard love," and the edifying arts uf the wire-puller and the log-roller (Laughter). Bat we need nit treat these blemishes too seriously. If, as we are told, laughter has a foreplace in life, still more has it a foreplace in ',elides, and I don't know how our hard-worked legielat ors at West- minster would get on without the relief of a little humour to oil the wheels of the Parliamentary machine. Personally I know my Conservative friends are much given to chaffing one. Wall, if you will excuse a IWe homely metaphor, I always find it the best plan when they pull my leg to pull theire. (Laughter.) All this is on the surface. and counts for very little. Never forget that at the root of all Liberal politics lies John Locke's splendid mexim—" The end of Government is the good of man- kind." (Rear, hear.) That is the foundation stone on which the whole edifice of Liberalism is built. That i 9 what has inspired the great Liberal chiefs in the past with high purpose and lofty Idea—Burke and Chatham, Pitt IVO Fox in the 18th century, Peel and Cobden, Bright and GI idstorie in the 19'h,statesme.n whose names are the glory of our modern political history. May I, in conclusion, quote you a passage from one of their Successors, a man on whose shoulders, as has been truly said, the mantle of Bright and Gladstone has fallen, a noble passage in itself which yields a still deeper signific- ance when we consider the life work, the strenuous endeavour, the heroic self-abnegation—if I may borrow one of his own phrases—of the scholar and statesman who penned it. He had been referring, I think, to the inevitable effect of seience in modifying the older forms of belief, and then in this memorable passage, the eloqu &amp;lee of which is the greater by contrast with habitual restraint, he concludes as follows :—" That swelling consciousness of height and freedom which we find in the older religions may still remain ; for how shall the universe ever cease to be a sovereign wonder of over- whelming power and superhuman fixedness of law ? And a man is already in no mean paradise, if at the hour of sunset a good hope can fall upon him, like harmonies of music, that the earth shall still be fair,and the happiness of every feeling creature still receive a constant augrnentation,and each good cause find worthy defendere, when the memory of his own poor name and personality has long been blotted out from the brief recollection of men for ever." (Anplaner). ADDRESS BY MISS ROBINSON. Miss Realms delivered an address on " Herne facts end foreign fancies." They were apt, she said, to lose sight of the end of Gevernmeet in the means used to obtain it. She was afraid that the good of mankind was not the object which all politicians had in view. Sue specially desired that women who took an interest ' in politics should keep the highest ideal before them. She had an opportunity of bearing Mr fins:atm give his lectnre on the problem of poverty. There were hundreds of thousands of people in this country who dli ant receive wages enough to keep them in physical efficiency. If they never spent one fraction of their small income on anything but food and clothing they Would not have enough to keep them in physical effiei- • ency. The result was seen in the absolute decline in the physique of the people, and with that went the want of proper interest in life,the want of proper training and a great deal that tended to make them morally and materially efficient. Such a fact as that called for the very highest and deepest thought that our politicians and statesmen could give. The drink bill had in- creased of late years notwithstanding the energies which had been put forth by temperance reforms's. The country was enorm nester wealthy, but still a Veit deal of poverty still existed and the problem was how to make a better division of wealth. They should do all they co old to lessen the awful evils which drink and gambling created, and which were sapping the vitality of the nation. The Educatien Bill would not tend very multi to improve education. An improved education I was a vital nsceseity for the children if they were to ' grow up to take the place their seniors desired them to take in the future. It was a fallacy to say that trade followed the flag, and that they were spreading civilization and Christianity by means of the sword in the distant parts of the World. All these were mistaken fallacies and led them into mistaken adventures and a 'waste of wealth and the end would be disastrous unlees then could get into right paths again. Another fallacy was that we ought to have a navy double the siz of any other navy in Europe. for foreign countries increased their navies in proportion as we increased ours. Let them look at the astounding way the military estimates had gone up. Half a century ago, the whole expendi- ture of this country was £51,000,000. Last year the exoenditure of the c-iuniiry had risen to £190,000,000 If the maney were spent for the welfare of humanity there would be nothing to say against it, but millions of money were being thrown away not for the b,nefit of humanity, but to enable us to destroy our neighbuura. We were now sending an expedition ageinst the Mad Mullah, but she was inclined to think that it was not the Mellah who was mad, but the British people. (Laughter,) The aop thrown to the public was that we were carrying Christianity and civilization to the dark places of the earth. Civilization and ChriAianicy might be intro- duced into the dark places of the earth without this terrible bloodshed ; indeed, she doubted very much whether they carried Christianity at the neint ef the sword, which was one of the most awful stumbling blocks to the real spread of Christianity. Wan it nrt a fact that we did not de what we professed ? Let them look at the condition of Ireland and of the people of India, Did the extreme poverty of the people of India speak well for the civilization we profeseed to give them. AB these questions required more study than they reeeived, and they woule be doing a wise thiag in getting clear end logical ideas of the sequence of events into their heads, and if they would do so they, would see that their primary duty was to attend to their own country and oppose these foreign expeditions, the expenditure on war and navies, and think more of the happiness and welfare of their own people. By setting up a really good and prosperous country at home we should be doing far more for the world at large than by all the great expeditions we could pessibly undertake. (Hear. hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mrs HOL'LES read the annual report, as follows :—"lra presenting our annual report we have to state that during the year your committee have met as usual. A successful and well patronized picnic took place in the summer at the popular country place ot Bollington. Delegates have attended the federation meetings held in London, the aaaociation being well represented by Mrs Clay and Mrs O'Brien. If time permitted no doubt they would be prepared with a shor resume of what t )olt place, but a very full report appeared in the daily papers at that time. We regret that we have not yet found a colloaguo f r Mr a Clay on the Board of Guardians, but your association is well represeniied on the Visiting Committee of the Workhouse. We would again ask our members to try and induce others to join, because it is Gale by associations of this kind that the interests and rights of women can be protected and pushed forward. New avocations for women are con- tinually springing up. Warrington has just lately ap- pointed a woman as sanitary inspecior. W 3 have to I report, with much regret, that, after many years of service, our troasurer 1369 resigned her position and Mrs Walker has kindly conswed to take it. It is highly satisfactory that Mrs (lune% who is a member of our assoeiation,was able to come forward and protest so ably and gracefully from a woman's point of view against the Education 8,1 l as it now stands before Parliament." (Applause). The report wee adopted. ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>Th CHAIRITAN proposA ttr. r.,-Aectinn of the follew- ing officers of the as4ociation for the ensuing year : Pr mident, Mrs Armitage ; vic -prssidents, bliss Dutton, Mrs Crnsfield, Mrs Garnett, Mrs Parker, and Mrs Pi( r• point ; tr, 3asursr, Mrs Walker ; rocretary, Mr r Holmes ; committee, Mrs Clay, Mrs Wylie, Mrs Hill, Mrs Rot), r:s, Mrs P Ratnbon,, Mr, O'Brien, Mrs Mlunfield, Mn 3 Simpson and Miss Crosfield.</p>
      </div>
