      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>TEMPERANCE MEETING AT</head>
        <p>TEMPERANCE MEETING AT</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE MEMORIAL BUILDINGS. The Rev: H. Dunnico and the Licensing Laws.</p>
        <p>A public temperance meeting was held under the auspices of the Wycliffe Temperance Society in the Memorial Buildings on Wednesday evening. The chair was taken by the Rev John YOnge and he was accompanied on the platform by the Rev Herbert Dunnico (Baptist minister), Mr B Farrar, and Mr F Morrall (hon secretary). An excellent programme of vocal and instrumental music,recita- tions, was given. The CHAIRMAN briefly introduced Mr Minnie° and extended to him a hearty welcome. The Rev H Duxxrco said he was pleased to mike the acquaintance of those cmuected with the Wycliffe Tem- perance Seciety aid he mast anngratltate them upon having such splendid rooms in which to meet. 'nest were met there as a temperance organization and if they were true to their object, they were banded together in the great battle against intemperance and strong drink. He need not emphasise the point that imtemperance was the great curse of the country, for it was the stumbling-block of all social and moral good. There was not a sinele man or woman who could I not point to many lives which had been ruined and lo:ig,hted in body and soul through the .cte of strong drink. Ile could not conceive of any man woo professed te be a follower of Christ, sitting down and being apothOle whilst this great curse was rampant in the land. If this stronghold Was to be removed it could I only be done hymen and women consecrated in the ser- vice of Jesus Chris'. Was it not a fact th at this drink curse was threatening to corrupt the political life of this country ? Some people told him that he was a little bit of a fanatic upon temperance reform but he simply looked upon it as one of the planks in the great gospel of Jesus Chriet, and it was desirable that they should coacentrate their energy upon it. It was threatening not only toe political life bu, the civic life of this country. No elecion could be fought to-day without taking into considerati id this important factor.' He had nothing tu say against the brewers and publicans in their individual capicitee. There were many men and women living good moral lives in public hoeses. As temperance workers they did not fight these people as individuals but as representatives of a trade, a ! trade that was threatening to blight the country. This ! trade had its organisations that existed solely to further its own interests. In these days when so many duties were being placed on the Town Councils 'such as the better housing of the paor,and the education of the community, it was necessary to have the most intelligent and best possible men on the Council,and yet these considerations were sub irdinved by the trade " to their cia-m interests. It did not matter how incapable a man might be for publie work, as long al tie was in sympathy witn "the trade " he received their money and help and all they c iuld give him in order to put him in authority. If temperance workers were to succe‘s- fu ly meet this opposition they must organise and place themselves behind temperance candidates and support them despite their political opinions. (Rear, hear.) They must insist upon the enforcement of the laws which they had managed to get from Parliament and agitate for new laws. It was quite as much a breach of the law to serve drink to a man until he was drunk as it was fora man to get drunk, and yet in Lancaenire alone in 1900 there were 36.000 convictions for drunkenness and only 160 or 170 publicans fined for permitting drunkenness. How were they to get the law put in force ? He suggested that a small committee of men of uobjassed miad and without strong politinal opinions, 'be formed cc take consider what steps should be taken to nublicans who offended against the licensing laws, to justice. When the laws were passed for the prevention of cruelty to animals and the preventio of cruelty to children, the societies concerned did not leave the enforcement of those laws in the hands of the police. They appointed their own inspectors ta go round and bring offenders to justice. It was no use getting laws pasted tor;the prevention of drunkenness if the entorcement of them was left entirely to the police. There were some towns where the police dare not enforce them. The Watch Committees were against any action being taken and reported Cases were taken no notice, of. They must have their own Temperance societies and their own inspectors. Proceeding, the speaker said they could do mach by individual effort. Organisation was worthless unless behiud that organisation they had the spirit of the Master. He appealed to all present to exercise the power of personal influence on behalf of the temperance cause. In alr Yonge they had a great temperance reformer and true Free Churchman. He (Mr Disunion) was in the Congregational body for many years under the Rev Thomas Hamer, and he knew Mr Yonge's name long before be saw him. He wished all possible success to the Wycliffe Temneranoe Society, and trusted that it would make its influence felt even in what was called this dronken town of Warrington. (Applause.) Mr Mosinexx (hen secretary) announced that in accordance with a decision of the committee,an enlarged photograph, saitablv framed, of the late Mr Tnomas Hawthorn, one of the vice-presidents of the eociety,had bcen Purchased out of the funds. On behalf of the Temperance Sooitry he had pleasure in asking the Chairman to accept it and to hang it in the Deacon's vestry.— The CHAIR.M&amp;N. in accepting the gift, paid a graceful tribute to the services rendered by the late Mr Hawthorn in colaeetion with Wycliffe Church and its numerous organisations. Mr JISEPH iIAWTHIRN thanked the members of the society for their kindly act. Mr B Faitamt delivered an address. He related to the pleasant surprise he had experienced in the present- ation of the portrait alluded to and proceeded to refer Ito the need of aggreisive temperance work. He alluded to the face that 80,000 people found untimely graves every year in this country through drink, and thought such a startling fact should lead them to promate total abstinence m-re than they had done in the past. lie knew many young men in %Varriagton who had been active Christian worker; in ties past but who were now careless and indifferent to religion through drink. There were 'many who were too ready to take incoxicatiog drink in times of sickness. The safest path was that of total abstinence.</p>
        <p>During the evening songs were capably sung by Messrs T [latch and J Corrin, and recit ttims were given with effect by Miss Owen and Mr Harupgon. Several selections were creditably played by a email instrumental band consisting of Misses F Vane, E Ward, Owen, &amp; Ward and L Turton. Miss Ward also contributed a violin solo and Miss Vane a mandoline solo, both efforts being greatly appreciated by the audience. A vote of thanks to all who bad taken part was passed on the proposition of Mr JOSEPH OWEN.</p>
