      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-14_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>ROMAN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>ROMAN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Interesting Lecture by Mr. T. May.</p>
        <p>" A Night in Riman Warringtod" was the subct Of an interesting lecture delivered to the members of the Warrington Society at the Old Academy on Thursday evening la•t week by Mr T May. Ccuncillor Bennett presided over a good attendancp.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said he thought Warrington was under an obligation to Mr May. The late Dr Ken- drick commenced the excavations for Roman remains at Wilderspool, but on his death any interest in the subjct seemed to have almost en- tirely evaporated, and for years people had almost forgotten that there was a Roman camp at Wilders- pool. It was reserved for a stranger—for Mr May came among them as a stranger—to re-discover the remains and follow up the excavations commenced by the late Dr Kendrick with an assiduity and patience which reflected all possible credit upon Mr May. At first even those who were supposed to be the custodians of the old associations of the town— the Museum Committee—were not as responsive as ' they ought to have been in regard to the excava- tions. Some who ought to have known better expressed doubt as to tha genuineness of the finds. Mr May, with the courage and patience of the true antiquarian,persevered in his labours and gradually won the sympathy of all whose sympathy was worth having and the active sympathy and sup- port of the Museum Committee. For six or seven years Mr May, although he was not a native of Warrington,had been good enough to keep his eye upon that most venerable relic of antiquity which remained in our midst. His enthusiasm and zeal bad been so great that on several Occasions he had giveu up his summer holidays in order to devote himself more assiduously to the work of eTcava- tion at Wilderspool. He had been rewarded by the appreciation and appro-al of the d:scerning public but what was a truer, better, and richer reward, came from the consciousnass of work well done and the enthusiasm which sprang from real zeal to pro- mote knowledge. _ .</p>
        <p>Mr MAY then proceeded with his lecture which was rendered additionally interesting by limelight views. He pointed out that the Romans when they subjugated Britain did not destroy the inhabitants. They disarmed them and set them Co work and one of their chief reasons for coming into Britain was to obtain possession of the very rich stores of ore in the shape of lead, cooper, zinc, and tin, which they highly valued. He thought that evening that he would convince his hearers that the remains which had been discovered at Wildsrspool were traces of the oldest industrial centre in great Britain. So far as he had been able to discover, there was not an encampment at Wilierspool but a fortified town, In a list of places in this country where Roman encampments and fortifications had existed, Warrington was described as Veratinum. He was inclined to think that the cor- rect name would probably have bean Feratinum which would mean the "iron town." because remains of iron smelting furnaces had been found at Wildersoool. A series of views of the excava- tions atWilderspool was exhibited and interestingly' described by the lecturer, A Roman road which had been discovered was construcred of gra velpiled up to a thickness of three feet, which showed, that the Ro-nans must have been must industrious and persevering, One of the most interesting relics which had been found was a foot rule, marked out in inches, which had no doubt been drooped by one of the Roman architects or masons. Photographs of several furnaces which have been unearthed were shown, Mr May being of the opinion that some were used for iron smelting, and one of them for roasting flints in order that glass might be made An unusuallr interesting fact was that some Coal had been found, mixed with sing and frag- ments of Roman pottery, which went to prove that the Romans used coal in smelting iron. Mr May described the Roman method of warming dwellings, which was evidenced by some ofl the remains of houses. A fire was lighted outside the house, and the heat was conducted underneath the floor and up the walls by means of flues. Photographs of a large number of relics were exhibited, one of them being a Roman altar which was probably' christianised about the year 386, the dedication to a heathen god having been erased,</p>
        <p>A hearty vote of thanks wag necorded to Mr Melt for his lecture, on the prJposition of Dr GonIYALL, seconded by Mr FULTON St.trrn, and supported by the Rev R Wriamrs Rs. Mr FILITEI, who ably manipulated the lantern, was also thanked.</p>
        <p>ROBBERY IN MANCHESTER.—EXPERT CBACKSMEN WORE.—Several large establishments in and about Picea dilly, Manchester, have recently shown signs of having been visited by burglars, though up to Wednes- day evening no big robbery had been reported. Over- night, however, expert eracksmen were evidently about for they forced an entry into thn establishment of Messrs Maoclonalds, Ltd.. dentists, Piccadilly, and got clear away with Porno £6O in money, and gold, etc., to the value of about £lOO. They had apparently entered the premises from the roof at the back, and reaching the office ransacked the whole place, the safe being. r.moved from its position and literally ripped open, whilst all the drawers were smashed open. From the nature of che operations no common thieves had been at work, though what they were doury to leave untouched platinum to the value of about £l,BOO is not readily explainable.</p>
